#DESCRIPTION "The Arcology Shell -- WP-001..WP-012 + WP-008/WP-009 + HELP + Profiles + Editors (RFC-0052)"

' RFC-0052 ("The Arcology Shell", see ../rfcs/RFC-0052_The_Arcology_Shell.md), WP-001 through
' WP-012, plus WP-008 (Display and Themes) and WP-009 (Plugin System), implemented out of numeric
' order alongside them, plus a built-in HELP command and RFC-0052 sections 20/22 (Prompt
' Architecture / Configuration -- see the "Profiles" section below, which also has "theme
' edit"/"prompt edit", interactive editors built on top of the same profile/theme/prompt commands)
' added directly on request -- none of the latter are one of RFC-0052's own numbered work
' packages. WP-009 (Plugin System) needed real research before implementation (see
' .agents/reports/ARCO_SH_RFC0052_WP009_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_RESEARCH.md) -- see the "Plugins" section
' below for the finding and the resulting design.
'
' WP-001 (shell skeleton): interactive loop, prompt, clean exit, current directory, diagnostic
' output, terminal capability query, explicit no-color mode.
'
' WP-002 (path layer): Path.IsArcology/Path.ToHost/Path.ToArcology/Path.Normalize below are the
' ONE Arcology colon-path <-> Linux host-path translation service (RFC section 7.3 -- "Path
' conversion MUST be implemented through one path service... Built-ins MUST NOT each implement
' colon-path parsing independently"). `cd` and the prompt are the only current consumers; anything
' added later that needs colon-path handling must call these, not re-parse ":segments:" itself.
'
' WP-003 (structured command parser): TokenizeCommand/ParseCommand below. Real Linux commands are
' parsed into a structure (Executable/Arguments/Redirections/PipelineStages/Background/Source),
' not executed by reparsing the raw source text.
'
' WP-004 (Linux process layer): Process.Execute (arco_runtime, src/runtime/runtime.cpp) does the
' real fork/execvp/waitpid work with correct foreground job-control terminal handoff. Superseded
' inside arcosh itself by WP-012's Process.StartJob/WaitJob below (real job control needs stop
' detection Process.Execute never had) but left in place, unchanged, as a simpler primitive for
' any OTHER ArcoBASIC program that just wants a one-shot blocking run with no job table.
'
' WP-005 (structured history + oops): MakeHistoryEntry/ClassifyProcessResult/RunOops below. Every
' non-empty command (built-ins included) appends one history entry; `oops <name>` replaces only the
' immediately-preceding COMMAND_NOT_FOUND command's executable, operating on its parsed structure
' (never its text), per RFC section 15.2's explicit "MUST NOT perform blind text replacement."
'
' WP-006 (resident ArcoBASIC program + immediate input): ParseLeadingLineNumber/SetProgramLine
' below implement the RFC section 8/9 numbered-line editor -- a line typed at the prompt that
' starts with a number is inserted/replaced/deleted (empty text) into an in-memory
' `resident_program` array kept in numeric order, never executed immediately. `LIST` prints it
' back; `RUN` reassembles it into source text and hands it to the new Runtime.RunString host
' function (arco_runtime, src/runtime/runtime.cpp), which compiles and executes it in a fresh,
' isolated interpreter -- so GOTO/line-number control flow works exactly as classic BASIC, without
' arcosh's own REPL loop having to interpret it; `NEW` clears the resident program. Section 8's
' "Input Classification" also requires "ArcoBASIC immediate input" as its own category, distinct
' from a shell command: IsArcoBasicImmediateInput/RunImmediateInput classify and run an unnumbered
' line typed directly at the prompt (`PRINT "hello"`, `x = 5`) via the new Runtime.EvalImmediate
' host function -- a SINGLE persistent interpreter for the whole session (unlike Runtime.RunString's
' deliberately fresh one per RUN), so a variable set on one line is still visible on the next, the
' way any real REPL/classic BASIC immediate mode behaves.
'
' WP-007 (script execution): RunScriptFile below. `RUN <file>` reads the raw file text and hands
' it directly to Runtime.RunString -- the SAME canonical compiler/parser path every other
' ArcoBASIC program goes through, never a hand-rolled preprocessor for numbered source
' (AP-0052-010's own explicit requirement). Numbered lines, unnumbered lines, and a mix of both in
' one file all work with zero special-casing here, since Parser::statement() itself already
' accepts an optional leading line-number label on any statement -- see
' arcosh/tests/fixtures/wp007_*.abas and RunScriptExecutionSelfTest below.
'
' WP-011 (pipelines and redirection): the fd-plumbing design (one process group per pipeline, real
' files opened for "<"/">"/">>") is now built into Process.StartJob (WP-012 below) directly; see
' its own comment in runtime.cpp. `$?`-equivalent semantics: a pipeline's own exit status is its
' LAST stage's, matching real shells; an earlier stage that fails to exec is reported on its own
' line ("command not found: ...") without changing that overall status.
'
' WP-012 (job control): Process.SetupShellJobControl/StartJob/WaitJob/PollJob/ContinueJob
' (arco_runtime, src/runtime/runtime.cpp -- see .agents/reports/
' ARCO_SH_RFC0052_WP012_JOB_CONTROL_RESEARCH.md for the full POSIX protocol these implement)
' replace WP-004/WP-011's Process.Execute/ExecutePipeline as the ONE real execution path for every
' external command arcosh runs, foreground or background: RunForegroundJob (real WUNTRACED stop
' detection -- Ctrl-Z on ANY foreground command, not just something explicitly backgrounded),
' RunBackgroundJob (`command &`, a real process group tracked in `jobs_table`, never a detached
' fire-and-forget child), PollBackgroundJobs (polled once per prompt line so a background job
' finishing/stopping on its own schedule is reported without ever blocking the prompt), and the
' `jobs`/`fg`/`bg` built-ins below.
'
' WP-008 (display and themes): AnsiEscape/DetectColorTier/DefaultTheme/StyleCode below implement
' RFC section 17's own literal API (Display.Style/Text/Line/Reset -- "Shell components SHOULD NOT
' emit arbitrary ANSI sequences directly", so every raw escape byte in this whole file comes from
' exactly these four functions, nowhere else) and section 18's themes (one bundled theme, the 8
' required semantic roles, TrueColor/256/16 fallbacks per role, collapsing to zero styling bytes
' at all for tier None -- AP-0052-011's own "all errors must remain understandable in monochrome
' output" requirement, satisfied by every message's own text already carrying its full meaning
' with color stripped out entirely). The prompt (`prompt`/`path` roles) and every `arcosh: ...`/
' job-control notice throughout this file now go through Display.Style or the small
' DisplayError/Warning/Success/Muted convenience wrappers instead of a bare PRINT.
'
' Still not implemented: plugins, completion -- later work packages (WP-009, WP-010).

FUNCTION HasFlag(flagList AS ARRAY, flagName AS STRING)
    FOR flag IN flagList
        IF flag == flagName THEN RETURN TRUE
    NEXT
    RETURN FALSE
END FUNCTION

' `--selftest-script-execution <fixtures-dir>` (WP-007) needs a value, not just presence, unlike
' every other --selftest-* flag -- the fixtures live as real files in the repo (arcosh/tests/
' fixtures/) rather than being synthesized in-memory, so the ctest invocation passes their
' directory as the argument right after the flag.
FUNCTION ArgumentAfterFlag(args AS ARRAY, flagName AS STRING)
    i = 0
    WHILE i < LEN(args)
        IF args[i] == flagName THEN
            IF i + 1 < LEN(args) THEN RETURN args[i + 1]
            RETURN ""
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Path Layer (RFC-0052 WP-002)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

FUNCTION Path.IsArcology(rawPath AS STRING)
    RETURN LEN(rawPath) > 0 ANDALSO String.Slice(rawPath, 0, 1) == ":"
END FUNCTION

' Strips surrounding whitespace and, if present, one enclosing pair of double quotes. Internal
' spaces inside a path segment (e.g. ":home:user:My Documents:") are left untouched.
FUNCTION Path.Normalize(rawPath AS STRING)
    text = String.Trim(rawPath)
    IF LEN(text) >= 2 THEN
        first = String.Slice(text, 0, 1)
        last = String.Slice(text, LEN(text) - 1, 1)
        IF first == "\"" ANDALSO last == "\"" THEN
            text = String.Slice(text, 1, LEN(text) - 2)
        END IF
    END IF
    RETURN text
END FUNCTION

' Shared by Path.ToHost and Path.ToArcology: drops empty segments (so "a::b" / "a//b" degrade to
' "a:b" / "a/b" instead of erroring) and collapses "." / ".." the same way for either separator.
FUNCTION PathCollapseSegments(rawSegments AS ARRAY)
    result = []
    FOR segment IN rawSegments
        IF segment == "" ORELSE segment == "." THEN
            CONTINUE FOR
        END IF

        IF segment == ".." THEN
            IF LEN(result) > 0 THEN ignored = Array.Pop(result)
            CONTINUE FOR
        END IF

        ignored = Array.Push(result, segment)
    NEXT
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

' :                     -> /
' :home:                -> /home/
' :home:user:           -> /home/user/
' :home:user:Desktop:   -> /home/user/Desktop/
' Non-Arcology input (no leading colon) passes through unchanged -- RFC section 7.2, ordinary
' Linux paths remain valid and are not this function's concern.
FUNCTION Path.ToHost(rawPath AS STRING)
    path = Path.Normalize(rawPath)
    IF !Path.IsArcology(path) THEN RETURN path

    body = String.Slice(path, 1, LEN(path))
    trailing = LEN(body) > 0 ANDALSO String.Slice(body, LEN(body) - 1, 1) == ":"
    IF trailing THEN body = String.Slice(body, 0, LEN(body) - 1)

    segments = PathCollapseSegments(String.Split(body, ":"))
    IF LEN(segments) == 0 THEN RETURN "/"

    result = "/" + String.Join(segments, "/")
    IF trailing THEN result = result + "/"
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

' Inverse of Path.ToHost. Non-absolute host input (no leading "/") passes through unchanged --
' there is no absolute Arcology path to map it to (RFC section 7.1: Arcology paths are absolute).
FUNCTION Path.ToArcology(rawPath AS STRING)
    path = Path.Normalize(rawPath)
    IF LEN(path) == 0 ORELSE String.Slice(path, 0, 1) <> "/" THEN RETURN path

    trailing = LEN(path) > 1 ANDALSO String.Slice(path, LEN(path) - 1, 1) == "/"
    body = String.Slice(path, 1, LEN(path))
    IF trailing THEN body = String.Slice(body, 0, LEN(body) - 1)

    segments = PathCollapseSegments(String.Split(body, "/"))
    IF LEN(segments) == 0 THEN RETURN ":"

    result = ":" + String.Join(segments, ":")
    IF trailing THEN result = result + ":"
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

' Styled per RFC-0052 section 17/18 (Display Abstraction/Themes, WP-008) -- degrading to the
' exact original plain text when the tier is None (piped stdin, --no-color, NO_COLOR, a dumb
' terminal, or simply no color-capable tty at all). A pure string-builder (StyleCode calls, never
' Console.Write) so the one caller still does a single write of the whole thing, matching how
' every other WP-002 Path.* function in this section stays a plain value-returning function.
'
' Composed from segments (RFC-0052 section 20, "Prompt Architecture" -- "The prompt SHOULD be
' composed from segments") rather than a single hardcoded format -- see ActivePromptSegments/
' RenderPromptSegment further down (in the "Profiles" section) for the actual segment list and
' what each one renders as; this function only joins whatever they produce with spaces and wraps
' the whole thing in the `prompt` role plus a trailing "> ". The DEFAULT segment list (["classic"])
' reproduces this exact function's ORIGINAL, pre-customization text byte-for-byte, so nobody who
' hasn't touched prompt customization ever sees their prompt change.
FUNCTION Prompt(cwd AS STRING, lastExitCode AS NUMBER, jobsTable AS ARRAY)
    tier = CurrentColorTier()
    resetCode = ""
    IF tier <> "None" THEN resetCode = AnsiEscape("0m")
    promptStyle = StyleCode("prompt", tier)
    context = MakePromptContext(cwd, lastExitCode, jobsTable, tier, resetCode, ActivePromptTags(), Host.CurrentUser())

    parts = []
    FOR segmentName IN ActivePromptSegments()
        rendered = RenderPromptSegment(segmentName, context)
        IF rendered <> "" THEN ignored = Array.Push(parts, rendered)
    NEXT

    RETURN promptStyle + String.Join(parts, " ") + resetCode + promptStyle + "> " + resetCode
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Structured Command Parser (RFC-0052 WP-003)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' Single/double-quote and backslash-escape aware word splitting (behavioral reference: the retired
' ../src/shell/arcosh.cpp's split_shell_words -- not ported, rewritten fresh here). Also emits
' "|", ">", ">>", "<", and "&" as their own tokens when they appear unquoted, rather than folding
' them into adjacent words, so ParseCommand below can actually see pipeline/redirection/background
' syntax instead of passing it through as literal argument text.
FUNCTION TokenizeCommand(text AS STRING)
    tokens = []
    current = ""
    in_single = FALSE
    in_double = FALSE
    escaping = FALSE
    i = 0
    length = LEN(text)

    WHILE i < length
        ch = String.Slice(text, i, 1)

        IF escaping THEN
            current = current + ch
            escaping = FALSE
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF ch == "\\" ANDALSO !in_single THEN
            escaping = TRUE
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF ch == "'" ANDALSO !in_double THEN
            in_single = !in_single
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF ch == "\"" ANDALSO !in_single THEN
            in_double = !in_double
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        is_space = FALSE
        IF ch == " " ORELSE ch == "\t" THEN is_space = TRUE
        IF is_space ANDALSO !in_single ANDALSO !in_double THEN
            IF current <> "" THEN
                ignored = Array.Push(tokens, current)
                current = ""
            END IF
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        is_operator = FALSE
        IF !in_single ANDALSO !in_double THEN
            IF ch == "|" ORELSE ch == ">" ORELSE ch == "<" ORELSE ch == "&" THEN
                is_operator = TRUE
            END IF
        END IF

        IF is_operator THEN
            IF current <> "" THEN
                ignored = Array.Push(tokens, current)
                current = ""
            END IF
            IF ch == ">" ANDALSO i + 1 < length ANDALSO String.Slice(text, i + 1, 1) == ">" THEN
                ignored = Array.Push(tokens, ">>")
                i = i + 2
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = Array.Push(tokens, ch)
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        current = current + ch
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    IF current <> "" THEN ignored = Array.Push(tokens, current)
    RETURN tokens
END FUNCTION

' Consumes TokenizeCommand's output into {Executable, Arguments, Redirections, PipelineStages,
' Background, Source} -- exactly WP-003's own field list. Executable/Arguments are always the
' FIRST pipeline stage (the plain, no-pipe case this shape originally described, unchanged);
' PipelineStages holds every stage AFTER the first, each a real {Executable, Arguments} object
' (RFC-0052 WP-011 -- before that pass this only recorded one "|" marker per pipe, structurally
' enough to detect and refuse a pipeline, not enough to actually run one). Redirections/Background
' are recognized here; background jobs are still refused at execution time (WP-012, not this
' pass), and redirection targets carry no `AS STRING`-vs-Arcology-path distinction of their own --
' RunCommand below resolves each Target through Path.ToHost just like everything else in this
' file does.
FUNCTION ParseCommand(text AS STRING)
    tokens = TokenizeCommand(text)
    redirections = []
    pipelineStages = []
    background = FALSE

    tokenCount = LEN(tokens)
    IF tokenCount > 0 THEN
        lastToken = tokens[tokenCount - 1]
        IF lastToken == "&" THEN
            background = TRUE
            ignored = Array.Pop(tokens)
            tokenCount = tokenCount - 1
        END IF
    END IF

    currentExecutable = ""
    currentArguments = []
    haveCurrentStage = FALSE

    i = 0
    WHILE i < tokenCount
        token = tokens[i]

        IF token == "|" THEN
            IF haveCurrentStage THEN
                ignored = Array.Push(pipelineStages, {"Executable": currentExecutable, "Arguments": currentArguments})
            END IF
            currentExecutable = ""
            currentArguments = []
            haveCurrentStage = FALSE
            i = i + 1
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF token == ">" ORELSE token == ">>" ORELSE token == "<" THEN
            target = ""
            IF i + 1 < tokenCount THEN target = tokens[i + 1]
            ignored = Array.Push(redirections, {"Operator": token, "Target": target})
            i = i + 2
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        haveCurrentStage = TRUE
        IF currentExecutable == "" THEN
            currentExecutable = token
        ELSE
            ignored = Array.Push(currentArguments, token)
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    IF haveCurrentStage THEN
        ignored = Array.Push(pipelineStages, {"Executable": currentExecutable, "Arguments": currentArguments})
    END IF

    executable = ""
    arguments = []
    IF LEN(pipelineStages) > 0 THEN
        first = pipelineStages[0]
        executable = first.Executable
        arguments = first.Arguments
        ignored = Array.RemoveAt(pipelineStages, 0)
    END IF

    RETURN {
        "Executable": executable,
        "Arguments": arguments,
        "Redirections": redirections,
        "PipelineStages": pipelineStages,
        "Background": background,
        "Source": text
    }
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Structured History + oops (RFC-0052 WP-005)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' RFC section 14's own field list, plus the parsed command's own structured fields so `oops` (and
' anything else that ever reads history) never needs to re-parse SourceText.
FUNCTION MakeHistoryEntry(parsed AS OBJECT, resultKind AS STRING, exitCode AS NUMBER)
    RETURN {
        "SourceText": parsed.Source,
        "Executable": parsed.Executable,
        "Arguments": parsed.Arguments,
        "Redirections": parsed.Redirections,
        "PipelineStages": parsed.PipelineStages,
        "Background": parsed.Background,
        "WorkingDirectory": Path.Cwd(),
        "Timestamp": Time.Timestamp(),
        "ResultKind": resultKind,
        "ExitCode": exitCode
    }
END FUNCTION

' Maps what WP-003/WP-004 can actually produce onto RFC section 14's result kinds. PLUGIN_FAILED/
' SCRIPT_FAILED have no producer yet (no plugin system, no script execution) -- not faked here.
FUNCTION ClassifyProcessResult(execResult AS OBJECT)
    IF !execResult.Found THEN
        IF String.Contains(execResult.Error, "Permission denied") THEN RETURN "PERMISSION_DENIED"
        RETURN "COMMAND_NOT_FOUND"
    END IF
    IF execResult.Signaled THEN RETURN "PROCESS_FAILED"
    IF execResult.ExitCode == 0 THEN RETURN "SUCCESS"
    RETURN "PROCESS_FAILED"
END FUNCTION

' Cosmetic reconstruction for display only (RFC 15.3: "clearly shows what command is being
' retried") -- oops/history print this, but execution always runs off the structured
' Executable/Arguments fields directly, never by re-parsing this string.
FUNCTION FormatCommandForDisplay(executable AS STRING, arguments AS ARRAY)
    parts = [executable]
    FOR a IN arguments
        formatted = a
        IF String.Contains(a, " ") THEN formatted = "\"" + a + "\""
        ignored = Array.Push(parts, formatted)
    NEXT
    RETURN String.Join(parts, " ")
END FUNCTION

' A full pipeline's display text -- every stage through FormatCommandForDisplay, joined the same
' way the real syntax would read. `stages` is the uniform {Executable, Arguments} shape
' BuildJobStages/RunOops both use (the first stage plus every PipelineStages entry after it).
FUNCTION FormatPipelineForDisplay(stages AS ARRAY)
    parts = []
    FOR stage IN stages
        ignored = Array.Push(parts, FormatCommandForDisplay(stage.Executable, stage.Arguments))
    NEXT
    RETURN String.Join(parts, " | ")
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Pipelines and Redirection (RFC-0052 WP-011)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' The LAST "<" redirection wins if a command somehow has more than one (matching real shells);
' "" means no input redirection was requested.
FUNCTION FindStdinRedirection(redirections AS ARRAY)
    target = ""
    FOR r IN redirections
        IF r.Operator == "<" THEN target = r.Target
    NEXT
    RETURN target
END FUNCTION

' The LAST ">" or ">>" wins, preserving whichever ONE of the two it actually was (unlike checking
' each operator separately and letting one unconditionally override the other regardless of
' which came later in the actual command text).
FUNCTION FindStdoutRedirection(redirections AS ARRAY)
    target = ""
    append = FALSE
    FOR r IN redirections
        IF r.Operator == ">" THEN
            target = r.Target
            append = FALSE
        END IF
        IF r.Operator == ">>" THEN
            target = r.Target
            append = TRUE
        END IF
    NEXT
    RETURN {"Target": target, "Append": append}
END FUNCTION

' Shared by RunForegroundJob/RunBackgroundJob: the full stage list (first stage + every
' PipelineStages entry after it -- ONE stage for a plain command with no "|" at all) and the
' resolved stdin/stdout redirection targets, everything Process.StartJob's own signature needs.
FUNCTION BuildJobStages(parsed AS OBJECT)
    stages = [{"Executable": parsed.Executable, "Arguments": parsed.Arguments}]
    FOR stage IN parsed.PipelineStages
        ignored = Array.Push(stages, stage)
    NEXT
    RETURN stages
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Job Control (RFC-0052 WP-012)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' Job numbers only ever increase for the life of the shell (never reused after a job finishes) --
' simpler than real shells' "reuse the lowest free number" convention and not worth the extra
' bookkeeping for this RFC's own minimal target list (jobs/fg/bg/suspend-resume), which says
' nothing about numbering reuse.
FUNCTION NextJobNumber(jobsTable AS ARRAY)
    highest = 0
    FOR job IN jobsTable
        IF job.Number > highest THEN highest = job.Number
    NEXT
    RETURN highest + 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION FindJobIndex(jobsTable AS ARRAY, jobNumber AS NUMBER)
    i = 0
    WHILE i < LEN(jobsTable)
        IF jobsTable[i].Number == jobNumber THEN RETURN i
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN -1
END FUNCTION

' `fg`/`fg %3`/`bg %3` all need "which job" resolved the same way: an explicit `%N` argument, or
' (bare `fg`/`bg`) the most recently added entry -- real shells call this the "current job".
FUNCTION MostRecentJobIndex(jobsTable AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(jobsTable) == 0 THEN RETURN -1
    RETURN LEN(jobsTable) - 1
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ResolveJobIndex(jobsTable AS ARRAY, arguments AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN RETURN MostRecentJobIndex(jobsTable)
    spec = arguments[0]
    IF LEN(spec) > 0 ANDALSO String.Slice(spec, 0, 1) == "%" THEN spec = String.Slice(spec, 1, LEN(spec))
    RETURN FindJobIndex(jobsTable, NUMBER(spec))
END FUNCTION

' Polled once per prompt line (RFC-0052 section 23's own "background jobs" requirement -- a
' background job finishing or stopping itself, e.g. it tried to read the terminal and got
' SIGTTIN'd, must be discovered without the shell ever blocking its own prompt to find out).
' Removes a job from the table the moment it's reported Done -- matching real shells, which show
' a finished background job's status exactly once, not on every subsequent `jobs`.
FUNCTION PollBackgroundJobs(jobsTable AS ARRAY)
    stillPending = []
    FOR job IN jobsTable
        poll = Process.PollJob(job.Pgid)
        IF poll.Changed THEN
            IF poll.Done THEN
                ignored = DisplayMuted("[" + STRING(job.Number) + "]+  Done                    " + job.Command)
            END IF
            IF poll.Stopped THEN
                ignored = DisplayWarning("[" + STRING(job.Number) + "]+  Stopped                 " + job.Command)
                job.State = "Stopped"
                ignored = Array.Push(stillPending, job)
            END IF
        ELSE
            ignored = Array.Push(stillPending, job)
        END IF
    NEXT
    ignored = Array.Resize(jobsTable, 0)
    FOR job IN stillPending
        ignored = Array.Push(jobsTable, job)
    NEXT
END FUNCTION

' The one real fork/pipe/dup2 entry point for EVERY external command this shell runs, foreground
' or background, single command or pipeline -- Process.StartJob already treats a plain command as
' a one-stage job (see its own comment), so there is no separate non-job-control code path left:
' WP-012's own "suspend/resume" requirement means Ctrl-Z has to work on an ORDINARY foreground
' command too (`sleep 100`, no "&"), not only on something explicitly backgrounded, so every
' foreground command needs the same WUNTRACED-aware wait this uses. Supersedes the old WP-004/
' WP-011 ExecuteAndRecord/ExecutePipelineAndRecord, which called the older, non-job-control
' Process.Execute/Process.ExecutePipeline directly -- those two host functions are unchanged and
' still exported for any OTHER ArcoBASIC program that just wants a one-shot blocking run with no
' job-table bookkeeping, but arcosh itself now needs the job-control-aware primitives everywhere.
FUNCTION RunForegroundJob(history AS ARRAY, jobsTable AS ARRAY, parsed AS OBJECT)
    stages = BuildJobStages(parsed)

    stdinTarget = FindStdinRedirection(parsed.Redirections)
    stdinPath = ""
    IF stdinTarget <> "" THEN stdinPath = Path.ToHost(stdinTarget)

    stdoutInfo = FindStdoutRedirection(parsed.Redirections)
    stdoutPath = ""
    appendStdout = FALSE
    IF stdoutInfo.Target <> "" THEN
        stdoutPath = Path.ToHost(stdoutInfo.Target)
        appendStdout = stdoutInfo.Append
    END IF

    started = Process.StartJob(stages, stdinPath, stdoutPath, appendStdout, TRUE)
    IF !started.Ok THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: " + started.Error)
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "PROCESS_FAILED", -1))
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    lastStageExecutable = stages[LEN(stages) - 1].Executable
    lastStageFound = TRUE
    lastStageError = ""
    FOR failure IN started.NotFound
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: command not found: " + failure.Executable)
        IF failure.Executable == lastStageExecutable THEN
            lastStageFound = FALSE
            lastStageError = failure.Error
        END IF
    NEXT

    waited = Process.WaitJob(started.Pgid, TRUE)

    IF waited.Stopped THEN
        jobNumber = NextJobNumber(jobsTable)
        ignored = Array.Push(jobsTable, {"Number": jobNumber, "Pgid": started.Pgid, "Command": parsed.Source, "State": "Stopped"})
        ignored = DisplayWarning("[" + STRING(jobNumber) + "]+  Stopped                 " + parsed.Source)
        ' 148 == 128 + SIGTSTP(20), matching real shells' own $? convention for a foreground job
        ' that stopped rather than exiting -- there is no real "exit code" yet (the job is still
        ' alive), but a caller checking `last_exit_code == 0` must see this as non-success.
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 148))
        RETURN 148
    END IF

    resultShape = {"Found": lastStageFound, "Signaled": waited.Signaled, "ExitCode": waited.ExitCode, "Error": lastStageError}
    resultKind = ClassifyProcessResult(resultShape)
    exitCode = -1
    IF lastStageFound THEN exitCode = waited.ExitCode

    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, resultKind, exitCode))
    RETURN exitCode
END FUNCTION

' `command &`. Starts the job without giving it the terminal and without waiting -- registers it
' in the job table immediately as Running and returns to the prompt, per AP-0052-015's explicit
' "Background execution MUST NOT be faked merely by spawning detached child processes" (this job
' has its own real process group and a live job-table entry PollBackgroundJobs can act on, not a
' fire-and-forget child arcosh loses track of).
FUNCTION RunBackgroundJob(history AS ARRAY, jobsTable AS ARRAY, parsed AS OBJECT)
    stages = BuildJobStages(parsed)

    stdinTarget = FindStdinRedirection(parsed.Redirections)
    stdinPath = ""
    IF stdinTarget <> "" THEN stdinPath = Path.ToHost(stdinTarget)

    stdoutInfo = FindStdoutRedirection(parsed.Redirections)
    stdoutPath = ""
    appendStdout = FALSE
    IF stdoutInfo.Target <> "" THEN
        stdoutPath = Path.ToHost(stdoutInfo.Target)
        appendStdout = stdoutInfo.Append
    END IF

    started = Process.StartJob(stages, stdinPath, stdoutPath, appendStdout, FALSE)
    IF !started.Ok THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: " + started.Error)
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "PROCESS_FAILED", -1))
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    FOR failure IN started.NotFound
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: command not found: " + failure.Executable)
    NEXT

    jobNumber = NextJobNumber(jobsTable)
    ignored = Array.Push(jobsTable, {"Number": jobNumber, "Pgid": started.Pgid, "Command": parsed.Source, "State": "Running"})
    ignored = DisplayMuted("[" + STRING(jobNumber) + "] " + STRING(started.Pgid))

    ' Real shells report `$?` as 0 immediately for `cmd &` regardless of what the job eventually
    ' does -- starting it successfully is the whole observable action of this statement.
    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 0))
    RETURN 0
END FUNCTION

' `jobs`: re-polls first so its own listing is never stale, then prints every remaining entry.
FUNCTION RunJobsCommand(jobsTable AS ARRAY)
    ignored = PollBackgroundJobs(jobsTable)
    FOR job IN jobsTable
        line = "[" + STRING(job.Number) + "]+  " + job.State + "                 " + job.Command
        IF job.State == "Stopped" THEN
            ignored = DisplayWarning(line)
        ELSE
            ignored = DisplayMuted(line)
        END IF
    NEXT
END FUNCTION

' `fg [%N]`: resumes (harmless no-op if it wasn't actually stopped) and gives the job the
' terminal, then blocks exactly like RunForegroundJob does for a brand-new command -- a job
' resumed to the foreground can stop AGAIN (another Ctrl-Z) just as validly as a fresh one can.
FUNCTION RunForegroundResume(history AS ARRAY, jobsTable AS ARRAY, arguments AS ARRAY)
    index = ResolveJobIndex(jobsTable, arguments)
    IF index < 0 THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: fg: no such job")
        RETURN -1
    END IF
    job = jobsTable[index]
    ignored = DisplayMuted(job.Command)
    ignored = Array.RemoveAt(jobsTable, index)

    resumed = Process.ContinueJob(job.Pgid, TRUE)
    IF !resumed.Ok THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: fg: " + resumed.Error)
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    waited = Process.WaitJob(job.Pgid, TRUE)
    IF waited.Stopped THEN
        ignored = DisplayWarning("[" + STRING(job.Number) + "]+  Stopped                 " + job.Command)
        ignored = Array.Push(jobsTable, {"Number": job.Number, "Pgid": job.Pgid, "Command": job.Command, "State": "Stopped"})
        RETURN 148
    END IF

    resultShape = {"Found": TRUE, "Signaled": waited.Signaled, "ExitCode": waited.ExitCode, "Error": ""}
    resultKind = ClassifyProcessResult(resultShape)
    exitCode = waited.ExitCode
    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry({"Source": job.Command, "Executable": "", "Arguments": [],
        "Redirections": [], "PipelineStages": [], "Background": FALSE}, resultKind, exitCode))
    RETURN exitCode
END FUNCTION

' `bg [%N]`: resumes a STOPPED job in the background -- unlike `fg`, never touches the terminal
' and never waits, matching RunBackgroundJob's own "start it and return to the prompt" shape.
FUNCTION RunBackgroundResume(jobsTable AS ARRAY, arguments AS ARRAY)
    index = ResolveJobIndex(jobsTable, arguments)
    IF index < 0 THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: bg: no such job")
        RETURN
    END IF
    job = jobsTable[index]
    IF job.State <> "Stopped" THEN
        ignored = DisplayWarning("arcosh: bg: job " + STRING(job.Number) + " is already running")
        RETURN
    END IF

    resumed = Process.ContinueJob(job.Pgid, FALSE)
    IF !resumed.Ok THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: bg: " + resumed.Error)
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = Array.RemoveAt(jobsTable, index)
    ignored = Array.Push(jobsTable, {"Number": job.Number, "Pgid": job.Pgid, "Command": job.Command, "State": "Running"})
    ignored = DisplayMuted("[" + STRING(job.Number) + "]+ " + job.Command + " &")
END FUNCTION

' AP-0052-015's own "do not fake it" bar, same standard AP-0052-014 set for pipelines: real
' processes, real signals, real waitpid -- structural checks alone are insufficient. ctest has no
' controlling terminal, so a real Ctrl-Z can't be simulated here (that part is verified manually
' against the real compiled binary under a real pty -- see arcosh/README.md's own WP-012 section
' for the transcript) -- but Process.WaitJob's WUNTRACED stop detection doesn't care WHICH signal
' caused the stop, so a real external `kill -STOP` against a job's own process group exercises the
' EXACT same stop/resume code path a real Ctrl-Z would, deterministically and without a terminal.
FUNCTION RunJobControlSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    history = []
    jobsTable = []

    ' Background job via the real "&" parser path, polled until it actually finishes.
    backgroundParsed = ParseCommand("sleep 0.2 &")
    backgroundExitCode = RunBackgroundJob(history, jobsTable, backgroundParsed)
    IF backgroundExitCode == 0 ANDALSO LEN(jobsTable) == 1 THEN
        PRINT "ok   background job started and registered in the job table"
    ELSE
        failMessage = "FAIL background job start: exitCode=" + STRING(backgroundExitCode)
        failMessage = failMessage + " table size=" + STRING(LEN(jobsTable))
        PRINT failMessage
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    pollCount = 0
    WHILE LEN(jobsTable) > 0 ANDALSO pollCount < 100
        ignored = PollBackgroundJobs(jobsTable)
        IF LEN(jobsTable) > 0 THEN Sleep(10)
        pollCount = pollCount + 1
    WEND
    IF LEN(jobsTable) == 0 THEN
        PRINT "ok   background job finished and was reaped by polling"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL background job still pending after " + STRING(pollCount) + " polls"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Suspend/resume against a real job, via Process.StartJob/WaitJob/ContinueJob directly (the
    ' same primitives RunForegroundJob/fg/bg wrap).
    stopStages = [{"Executable": "sleep", "Arguments": ["2"]}]
    stopStarted = Process.StartJob(stopStages, "", "", FALSE, FALSE)
    IF !stopStarted.Ok THEN
        PRINT "FAIL could not start suspend/resume test job: " + stopStarted.Error
        failures = failures + 1
    ELSE
        ignored = Process.Execute("kill", ["-STOP", "-" + STRING(stopStarted.Pgid)])
        Sleep(100)
        stopWaited = Process.WaitJob(stopStarted.Pgid, FALSE)
        IF stopWaited.Stopped THEN
            PRINT "ok   an external stop signal was detected as a job stop (WUNTRACED)"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL job was not detected as stopped (Done=" + STRING(stopWaited.Done) + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF

        stopResumed = Process.ContinueJob(stopStarted.Pgid, FALSE)
        IF !stopResumed.Ok THEN
            PRINT "FAIL Process.ContinueJob failed: " + stopResumed.Error
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF

        Sleep(100)
        stillRunning = Process.PollJob(stopStarted.Pgid)
        IF !stillRunning.Done THEN
            PRINT "ok   job kept running after being resumed, not falsely reported done"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL job reported done almost immediately after resume"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF

        resumeWaitCount = 0
        WHILE !stillRunning.Done ANDALSO resumeWaitCount < 200
            Sleep(20)
            stillRunning = Process.PollJob(stopStarted.Pgid)
            resumeWaitCount = resumeWaitCount + 1
        WEND
        IF stillRunning.Done ANDALSO stillRunning.ExitCode == 0 THEN
            PRINT "ok   job completed normally after being resumed"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL job did not complete normally after resume (Done=" + STRING(stillRunning.Done) + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    END IF

    ' `bg` on a genuinely stopped job.
    bgStages = [{"Executable": "sleep", "Arguments": ["0.3"]}]
    bgStarted = Process.StartJob(bgStages, "", "", FALSE, FALSE)
    ignored = Process.Execute("kill", ["-STOP", "-" + STRING(bgStarted.Pgid)])
    Sleep(100)
    ignored = Process.WaitJob(bgStarted.Pgid, FALSE)
    bgJobsTable = []
    ignored = Array.Push(bgJobsTable, {"Number": 1, "Pgid": bgStarted.Pgid, "Command": "sleep 0.3", "State": "Stopped"})
    ignored = RunBackgroundResume(bgJobsTable, [])
    IF LEN(bgJobsTable) == 1 THEN
        IF bgJobsTable[0].State == "Running" THEN
            PRINT "ok   bg resumed a stopped job into the Running state"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL bg did not mark the job Running (state=" + bgJobsTable[0].State + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL bg changed the job table size unexpectedly (" + STRING(LEN(bgJobsTable)) + ")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    bgPollCount = 0
    WHILE LEN(bgJobsTable) > 0 ANDALSO bgPollCount < 100
        ignored = PollBackgroundJobs(bgJobsTable)
        IF LEN(bgJobsTable) > 0 THEN Sleep(10)
        bgPollCount = bgPollCount + 1
    WEND
    IF LEN(bgJobsTable) == 0 THEN
        PRINT "ok   bg-resumed job finished and was reaped"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL bg-resumed job never finished"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' AP-0052-014's own explicit rule: "Tests MUST inspect actual execution/output behavior. Parser-
' only tests are insufficient." Every case here really forks/pipes/execs real Linux programs
' (cat/grep/wc) and reads back a REAL file the pipeline/redirection actually wrote, rather than
' just checking ParseCommand's parsed structure -- that's already covered by ParseCommand being
' plain, structure-only ArcoBASIC, no need to re-test it here.
FUNCTION RunPipelineSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    history = []
    jobsTable = []
    base = Path.Cwd()
    inputPath = base + "/wp011_selftest_input.txt"
    outPath = base + "/wp011_selftest_output.txt"
    ignored = File.WriteText(inputPath, "apple\nbanana\ncherry\navocado\n")

    ' Real pipeline: cat | grep -c, output redirected so this can read back the REAL count grep
    ' itself computed, not something ArcoBASIC derived independently.
    ignored = File.WriteText(outPath, "")
    parsed1 = ParseCommand("cat " + inputPath + " | grep -c a > " + outPath)
    exitCode1 = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed1)
    out1 = String.Trim(File.ReadText(outPath))
    IF exitCode1 == 0 ANDALSO out1 == "3" THEN
        PRINT "ok   pipeline (cat | grep -c) exit=0 output=" + out1
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL pipeline (cat | grep -c) exit=" + STRING(exitCode1) + " output=[" + out1 + "] (expected exit=0 output=3)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Plain redirection, no pipe at all: wc -l > file.
    ignored = File.WriteText(outPath, "")
    parsed2 = ParseCommand("wc -l " + inputPath + " > " + outPath)
    exitCode2 = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed2)
    out2 = File.ReadText(outPath)
    IF exitCode2 == 0 ANDALSO String.Contains(out2, "4") THEN
        PRINT "ok   redirection (wc -l > file) exit=0 output contains 4"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL redirection (wc -l > file) exit=" + STRING(exitCode2) + " output=[" + out2 + "] (expected exit=0, output containing 4)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Append: run the SAME redirect twice with >>, the real file on disk must show it happened
    ' twice -- not something a mocked/faked redirection could get right by accident.
    appendPath = base + "/wp011_selftest_append.txt"
    ignored = File.WriteText(appendPath, "")
    parsed3a = ParseCommand("cat " + inputPath + " >> " + appendPath)
    ignored = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed3a)
    parsed3b = ParseCommand("cat " + inputPath + " >> " + appendPath)
    ignored = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed3b)
    appended = File.ReadText(appendPath)
    appendedLines = String.Split(appended, "\n")
    IF LEN(appendedLines) == 9 THEN
        PRINT "ok   append (>>) twice produced 9 newline-separated fields (4 lines x2 + trailing)"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL append (>>) produced " + STRING(LEN(appendedLines)) + " fields (expected 9)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Input redirection combined with output redirection in the SAME command: wc -l < file > file.
    ignored = File.WriteText(outPath, "")
    parsed4 = ParseCommand("wc -l < " + inputPath + " > " + outPath)
    exitCode4 = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed4)
    out4 = String.Trim(File.ReadText(outPath))
    IF exitCode4 == 0 ANDALSO out4 == "4" THEN
        PRINT "ok   combined input+output redirection (wc -l < file > file) == 4"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL combined input+output redirection == [" + out4 + "] exit=" + STRING(exitCode4) + " (expected 4, exit 0)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' An earlier pipeline stage failing to exec must not change the pipeline's OWN exit status
    ' (matching real shell semantics -- see RunForegroundJob's own comment) while still being
    ' reported.
    parsed5 = ParseCommand("totally-not-a-real-command-arcosh-wp011 | cat")
    exitCode5 = RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, parsed5)
    IF exitCode5 == 0 THEN
        PRINT "ok   pipeline survives an earlier stage's command-not-found, exit == 0"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL pipeline exit after earlier command-not-found == " + STRING(exitCode5) + " (expected 0)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF LEN(history) == 6 THEN
        PRINT "ok   each pipeline/redirection command recorded exactly one history entry"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL history has " + STRING(LEN(history)) + " entries (expected 6)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' RFC section 15, version 1 rules: applies only to the immediately preceding command, requires
' that command to have failed with COMMAND_NOT_FOUND, preserves arguments/redirections/pipeline
' structure/quoting semantics by construction (it operates on the parsed STRUCTURE, never on
' text -- see FormatCommandForDisplay's own comment), and replaces only the executable field --
' but never necessarily the FIRST stage's: a pipeline's own ResultKind always reflects its LAST
' stage (matching real shell `$?` semantics -- see RunForegroundJob), so COMMAND_NOT_FOUND can
' only ever be reached when the LAST stage is the one that actually failed to exec. This targets
' that stage specifically -- the only stage for a plain command with no pipeline at all, or the
' final PipelineStages entry otherwise -- replacing just its executable and keeping its own
' arguments, while every earlier stage (a different command entirely, e.g. `cat file | gerp -c o`
' -- `cat file` is untouched, only `gerp -c o` gets fixed) passes through unchanged.
FUNCTION RunOops(history AS ARRAY, jobsTable AS ARRAY, oopsCommand AS OBJECT)
    IF LEN(oopsCommand.Arguments) == 0 THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: oops: usage: oops <command>")
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(oopsCommand, "INVALID_SYNTAX", -1))
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    IF LEN(history) == 0 THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: oops: no previous command")
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(oopsCommand, "INVALID_SYNTAX", -1))
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    previous = history[LEN(history) - 1]
    IF previous.ResultKind <> "COMMAND_NOT_FOUND" THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: oops: previous command did not fail with command not found")
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(oopsCommand, "INVALID_SYNTAX", -1))
        RETURN -1
    END IF

    newExecutable = oopsCommand.Arguments[0]

    fixedExecutable = previous.Executable
    fixedArguments = previous.Arguments
    fixedPipelineStages = []

    stageCount = LEN(previous.PipelineStages)
    IF stageCount == 0 THEN
        fixedExecutable = newExecutable
    ELSE
        i = 0
        WHILE i < stageCount
            stage = previous.PipelineStages[i]
            IF i == stageCount - 1 THEN
                ignored = Array.Push(fixedPipelineStages, {"Executable": newExecutable, "Arguments": stage.Arguments})
            ELSE
                ignored = Array.Push(fixedPipelineStages, stage)
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
    END IF

    allStages = [{"Executable": fixedExecutable, "Arguments": fixedArguments}]
    FOR stage IN fixedPipelineStages
        ignored = Array.Push(allStages, stage)
    NEXT
    displayText = FormatPipelineForDisplay(allStages)
    ignored = DisplayMuted("arcosh: retrying: " + displayText)
    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(oopsCommand, "SUCCESS", 0))

    retried = {
        "Executable": fixedExecutable,
        "Arguments": fixedArguments,
        "Redirections": previous.Redirections,
        "PipelineStages": fixedPipelineStages,
        "Background": previous.Background,
        "Source": displayText
    }
    RETURN RunForegroundJob(history, jobsTable, retried)
END FUNCTION

' Structural regression test for RFC-0052 AP-0052-008's own required scenario -- drives the exact
' same MakeHistoryEntry/RunOops logic the real REPL uses, without spawning real processes, so it
' proves the STRUCTURAL guarantee (only Executable changes; a quoted argument that happens to
' contain the failed executable's own name is untouched) independent of any real "git"/"gti"
' binary's behavior.
FUNCTION RunOopsSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    history = []

    failed = ParseCommand("gti commit -m \"gti must remain inside this message\"")
    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(failed, "COMMAND_NOT_FOUND", -1))

    oopsCommand = ParseCommand("oops git")
    IF LEN(oopsCommand.Arguments) == 0 THEN
        PRINT "FAIL oops parsed with no argument"
        failures = failures + 1
        RETURN failures
    END IF

    newExecutable = oopsCommand.Arguments[0]
    previous = history[LEN(history) - 1]

    IF newExecutable == "git" THEN
        PRINT "ok   oops replacement executable == git"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL oops replacement executable == " + newExecutable + " (expected git)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    expectedArgs = ["commit", "-m", "gti must remain inside this message"]
    IF LEN(previous.Arguments) == LEN(expectedArgs) THEN
        PRINT "ok   preserved argument count == " + STRING(LEN(expectedArgs))
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL preserved argument count == " + STRING(LEN(previous.Arguments)) + " (expected " + STRING(LEN(expectedArgs)) + ")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    i = 0
    WHILE i < LEN(expectedArgs)
        actual = ""
        IF i < LEN(previous.Arguments) THEN actual = previous.Arguments[i]
        IF actual == expectedArgs[i] THEN
            PRINT "ok   argument " + STRING(i) + " == " + expectedArgs[i]
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL argument " + STRING(i) + " == " + actual + " (expected " + expectedArgs[i] + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    displayText = FormatCommandForDisplay(newExecutable, previous.Arguments)
    expectedDisplay = "git commit -m \"gti must remain inside this message\""
    IF displayText == expectedDisplay THEN
        PRINT "ok   display text == " + displayText
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL display text == " + displayText + " (expected " + expectedDisplay + ")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' A real, end-to-end regression for the multi-stage-pipeline bug found via manual testing: the
    ' failing stage in a pipeline is always the LAST one (ClassifyProcessResult's COMMAND_NOT_FOUND
    ' can only come from there -- see RunOops's own comment), so this drives RunOops for REAL (real
    ' fork/exec, not just structure) against a pipeline whose FIRST stage's own argument happens to
    ' contain the failing executable's name -- proving both that the right (last) stage gets fixed
    ' AND that the unrelated earlier stage survives completely untouched, extending the
    ' single-command guarantee above to pipelines.
    jobsTableForPipelineOops = []
    pipelineFailed = {
        "Executable": "grep",
        "Arguments": ["gerp must survive this message", "missing-file-for-oops-selftest.txt"],
        "Redirections": [],
        "PipelineStages": [{"Executable": "gerp", "Arguments": ["-c", "x"]}],
        "Background": FALSE,
        "Source": "grep \"gerp must survive this message\" missing-file-for-oops-selftest.txt | gerp -c x"
    }
    ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(pipelineFailed, "COMMAND_NOT_FOUND", -1))

    pipelineOopsCommand = ParseCommand("oops grep")
    ignored = RunOops(history, jobsTableForPipelineOops, pipelineOopsCommand)

    fixedEntry = history[LEN(history) - 1]
    expectedFixedSource = "grep \"gerp must survive this message\" missing-file-for-oops-selftest.txt | grep -c x"
    IF fixedEntry.SourceText == expectedFixedSource THEN
        PRINT "ok   pipeline oops fixed only the failing (last) stage: " + fixedEntry.SourceText
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL pipeline oops produced: " + fixedEntry.SourceText + " (expected " + expectedFixedSource + ")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Not asserting a specific exit code -- the fixed second stage is a real `grep -c x` with
    ' nothing on its stdin to match (the first stage's own file genuinely doesn't exist), so its
    ' real exit status is grep's own "no match" (1), not "success" (0). What matters here is that
    ' it was FOUND and ran at all, proving the fix, not what it found.
    IF fixedEntry.ResultKind <> "COMMAND_NOT_FOUND" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the corrected pipeline's last stage was found and actually ran"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL the corrected pipeline still reports COMMAND_NOT_FOUND"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Display and Themes (RFC-0052 WP-008)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' RFC section 17: "Shell components SHOULD NOT emit arbitrary ANSI sequences directly" -- every
' raw escape sequence in this whole file is built and emitted from exactly the four Display.*
' functions below, never inline elsewhere (arcosh's own prompt/error/job-notice call sites below
' all go through Display.Style/Text/Line/Reset, per that same section's own example).
FUNCTION AnsiEscape(code AS STRING)
    RETURN Chr(27) + "[" + code
END FUNCTION

' RFC section 18: themes "SHOULD provide fallback mappings for: 24-bit / 256-color / 16-color /
' no-color". Detected once at startup (arcosh has no runtime theme-switching command in this
' pass -- RFC section 22, Configuration, is future work), not re-checked per Display.* call.
' `COLORTERM=truecolor`/`24bit` is the real-world signal terminals set for 24-bit support; a
' `TERM` containing "256color" (xterm-256color, screen-256color, ...) is the standard signal for
' 256-color support; anything else that reaches here (a real tty, not explicitly disabled, not
' "dumb") gets the universally-supported 16-color ANSI codes. `isTty`/`noColorFlag`/`noColorEnv`
' are host-returned bools passed in as plain parameters and only ever used in bare `IF`/`IF !`
' here -- never ANDALSO/ORELSE'd directly -- for the same disclosed reason the startup is_tty/no_color_flag/no_color_env
' detection near the bottom of this file does the same.
FUNCTION DetectColorTier(isTty AS BOOL, noColorFlag AS BOOL, noColorEnv AS BOOL, term AS STRING, colorTerm AS STRING)
    IF !isTty THEN RETURN "None"
    IF noColorFlag THEN RETURN "None"
    IF noColorEnv THEN RETURN "None"
    IF term == "dumb" ORELSE term == "" THEN RETURN "None"
    IF colorTerm == "truecolor" ORELSE colorTerm == "24bit" THEN RETURN "TrueColor"
    IF String.Contains(term, "256color") THEN RETURN "Color256"
    RETURN "Color16"
END FUNCTION

' The one bundled theme AP-0052-011 requires ("create at least one bundled theme proving the
' interface") -- the 8 required semantic roles, each mapped to a ready-to-use SGR parameter
' string per RFC section 18's own three color-capable tiers (`None` needs no entry: StyleCode
' below never looks one up for it). `normal` resets rather than picking a color, so
' `Display.Style("normal")` doubles as "stop styling" without a separate call. `selection` is
' reverse video (SGR 7) -- tier-independent, the same code in all three tiers, since it inverts
' whatever the terminal's own default colors already are rather than picking new ones.
FUNCTION DefaultTheme()
    RETURN {
        "normal":    {"TrueColor": "0", "Color256": "0", "Color16": "0"},
        "prompt":    {"TrueColor": "1;38;2;97;175;239", "Color256": "1;38;5;75", "Color16": "1;34"},
        "path":      {"TrueColor": "38;2;86;182;194", "Color256": "38;5;73", "Color16": "36"},
        "success":   {"TrueColor": "38;2;98;209;150", "Color256": "38;5;71", "Color16": "32"},
        "warning":   {"TrueColor": "38;2;229;192;123", "Color256": "38;5;179", "Color16": "33"},
        "error":     {"TrueColor": "1;38;2;224;108;117", "Color256": "1;38;5;167", "Color16": "1;31"},
        "muted":     {"TrueColor": "2;38;2;150;150;150", "Color256": "2;38;5;244", "Color16": "2"},
        "selection": {"TrueColor": "7", "Color256": "7", "Color16": "7"}
    }
END FUNCTION

' "" for an unrecognized role -- Display.Style silently no-ops for it (see its own comment on
' why that beats panicking over a styling call, of all things).
FUNCTION StyleCode(role AS STRING, tier AS STRING)
    IF tier == "None" THEN RETURN ""
    roleEntry = Object.Get(ActiveTheme(), role)
    IF TYPEOF(roleEntry) <> "Object" THEN RETURN ""
    code = Object.Get(roleEntry, tier)
    IF code == "" THEN RETURN ""
    RETURN AnsiEscape(code + "m")
END FUNCTION

' Session-wide color tier, set once at startup (see the main script body below) and read by every
' Display.* call via Runtime.GetGlobal/SetGlobal -- the documented, host-provided mechanism for
' exactly this "a function needs to read state a totally different part of the program set"
' need (the same primitive AstAmirBuilder::apply_script_global_scoping itself is built on, per
' Runtime.GetGlobal's own comment in runtime.cpp), rather than threading a tier parameter through
' every single call site (and RFC section 17's own example -- `Display.Text(...)` then
' `Display.Style(...)` then `Display.Line(...)` then `Display.Reset()` -- takes exactly one
' argument each, not a tier parameter on top).
FUNCTION CurrentColorTier()
    tier = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_color_tier")
    IF tier == "" THEN RETURN "None"
    RETURN tier
END FUNCTION

' Display.Style/Text/Line/Reset -- RFC section 17's own literal API. Deliberately never fails on
' an unrecognized role (falls through StyleCode's own "" case to a no-op): a styling call is
' cosmetic by construction, and AP-0052-011's own "all errors must remain understandable in
' monochrome output" rule means the actual MEANING of any message these wrap must already be
' carried entirely by its own text -- a missing/wrong color is a coloring bug, never a
' comprehension one, so it should never be treated as an error condition capable of hiding or
' replacing that text.
FUNCTION Display.Style(role AS STRING)
    ignored = Console.Write(StyleCode(role, CurrentColorTier()))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Display.Text(text AS STRING)
    ignored = Console.Write(text)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Display.Line(text AS STRING)
    PRINT text
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION Display.Reset()
    tier = CurrentColorTier()
    IF tier <> "None" THEN ignored = Console.Write(AnsiEscape("0m"))
END FUNCTION

' Small convenience wrappers used throughout the rest of this file wherever a single styled line
' needs to be printed and immediately reset -- every one of these still reads as a plain,
' complete, meaningful sentence with the color stripped out entirely (AP-0052-011's own
' monochrome requirement), matching the existing "arcosh: ..." message text this project already
' uses verbatim, just now optionally colored.
FUNCTION DisplayStyledLine(role AS STRING, text AS STRING)
    ignored = Display.Style(role)
    ignored = Display.Line(text)
    ignored = Display.Reset()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DisplayError(text AS STRING)
    ignored = DisplayStyledLine("error", text)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DisplayWarning(text AS STRING)
    ignored = DisplayStyledLine("warning", text)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DisplaySuccess(text AS STRING)
    ignored = DisplayStyledLine("success", text)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DisplayMuted(text AS STRING)
    ignored = DisplayStyledLine("muted", text)
END FUNCTION

' Structural + real-output regression coverage. `--no-color`/`NO_COLOR`/non-tty all collapsing to
' tier "None" is already covered by the pre-existing is_tty/no_color_flag/no_color_env detection
' this reuses the same signals as; what's new here is the theme/tier -> actual ANSI bytes mapping.
FUNCTION RunDisplaySelfTest()
    failures = 0

    IF DetectColorTier(FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, "xterm-256color", "") == "None" THEN
        PRINT "ok   no tty forces tier None regardless of TERM/COLORTERM"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL no tty did not force tier None"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF DetectColorTier(TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, "xterm-256color", "truecolor") == "None" THEN
        PRINT "ok   --no-color forces tier None even with COLORTERM=truecolor"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL --no-color did not force tier None"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF DetectColorTier(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, "dumb", "") == "None" THEN
        PRINT "ok   TERM=dumb forces tier None"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL TERM=dumb did not force tier None"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF DetectColorTier(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, "xterm", "truecolor") == "TrueColor" THEN
        PRINT "ok   COLORTERM=truecolor selects tier TrueColor"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL COLORTERM=truecolor did not select tier TrueColor"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF DetectColorTier(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, "xterm-256color", "") == "Color256" THEN
        PRINT "ok   TERM containing 256color selects tier Color256"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL TERM=xterm-256color did not select tier Color256"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF DetectColorTier(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, "xterm", "") == "Color16" THEN
        PRINT "ok   an ordinary tty with no other signal selects tier Color16"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a plain tty did not select tier Color16"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    requiredRoles = ["normal", "prompt", "path", "success", "warning", "error", "muted", "selection"]
    theme = DefaultTheme()
    FOR role IN requiredRoles
        entry = Object.Get(theme, role)
        IF TYPEOF(entry) == "Object" THEN
            hasAllTiers = Object.Has(entry, "TrueColor")
            IF hasAllTiers THEN hasAllTiers = Object.Has(entry, "Color256")
            IF hasAllTiers THEN hasAllTiers = Object.Has(entry, "Color16")
            IF hasAllTiers THEN
                PRINT "ok   theme role " + role + " has TrueColor/Color256/Color16 fallbacks"
            ELSE
                PRINT "FAIL theme role " + role + " is missing a tier"
                failures = failures + 1
            END IF
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL theme is missing required role " + role
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    NEXT

    expectedError = AnsiEscape("1;38;2;224;108;117m") + "!" + AnsiEscape("0m")
    actualError = StyleCode("error", "TrueColor") + "!" + AnsiEscape("0m")
    IF actualError == expectedError THEN
        PRINT "ok   StyleCode produces the real ANSI bytes for a known role/tier pair"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL StyleCode produced unexpected bytes for error/TrueColor"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF StyleCode("not-a-real-role", "TrueColor") == "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   an unrecognized role silently no-ops instead of failing"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL an unrecognized role did not no-op"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Real output, not just structure: Display.Reset must never emit anything when the tier is
    ' None -- an actual monochrome terminal (or a pipe, as ctest itself is) must see byte-for-byte
    ' plain text, never a stray escape code it can't render.
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_color_tier", "None")
    noneStyle = StyleCode("error", CurrentColorTier())
    IF noneStyle == "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   tier None produces zero style bytes for any role"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL tier None still produced style bytes"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Help (arcosh built-in -- not one of RFC-0052's own numbered work packages, added directly on
' request rather than tracked against an AP packet). Deliberately scoped to what ArcoSH ITSELF
' actually does today, real and accurate, never aspirational -- the same "never describe planned
' functionality as implemented" discipline RFC-0052 AP-0052-017 states for this project's external
' documentation, applied here too even though HELP isn't that AP's own subject. The topic table
' below is plain DATA (Name/Aliases/Summary/Body), not code, specifically so it can grow -- toward
' a fuller ArcoBASIC language reference, and eventually interactive step-by-step tutorials, both
' explicitly out of scope for this pass -- without RunHelpCommand/FindHelpTopic ever needing to
' change shape just because more topics get added later.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

FUNCTION HelpTopics()
    RETURN [
        {
            "Name": "help",
            "Aliases": ["?"],
            "Summary": "how to use HELP itself",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "help                 lists every topic below with a one-line summary.",
                "help <topic>         shows the full text for one topic.",
                "",
                "Topic names are case-insensitive, and some have a short alias shown in",
                "parentheses in the topic list -- \"help ?\" and \"help help\" are the same thing."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "overview",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "what ArcoSH is and how it differs from a POSIX shell",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "ArcoSH is a real Linux shell whose own scripting/automation language IS",
                "ArcoBASIC -- there is no separate shell-scripting dialect (RFC-0052 section 2.2).",
                "",
                "A plain command line (ls -la, git status | grep modified) runs a real Linux",
                "program, exactly like any other shell. A line that looks like ArcoBASIC instead",
                "(PRINT \"hello\", x = 5) runs immediately as ArcoBASIC -- see \"help immediate\".",
                "",
                "Other topics: paths, commands, pipelines, jobs, oops, history, program,",
                "immediate, run, themes, arcobasic."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "paths",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "Arcology colon-paths and how they relate to ordinary Linux paths",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Arcology paths use colons instead of slashes and are always absolute:",
                "",
                "    :               the root, equivalent to /",
                "    :home:          equivalent to /home/",
                "    :home:user:     equivalent to /home/user/",
                "",
                "cd accepts either kind of path (or no argument, which goes to your home",
                "directory); the prompt always shows the current directory as an Arcology path.",
                "Ordinary Linux paths (relative paths, or ones with no leading colon/slash) pass",
                "through unchanged either direction -- you never have to translate anything",
                "yourself, and real Linux commands still take ordinary Linux paths as arguments."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "commands",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "running real Linux programs, arguments, and quoting",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Anything that isn't classified as ArcoBASIC (see \"help immediate\") or one of",
                "ArcoSH's own built-ins is parsed into a real command and executed for real --",
                "fork/exec, not a text substitution. Single and double quotes and backslash",
                "escapes work the way you'd expect from any other shell for splitting arguments.",
                "",
                "If a command isn't found, ArcoSH says so plainly (\"command not found: x\") and",
                "records it in history so \"oops\" (see that topic) can retry it. There is no",
                "$PATH-search ambiguity to worry about beyond the ordinary PATH environment",
                "variable, exactly like any other shell."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "pipelines",
            "Aliases": ["redirection"],
            "Summary": "real | pipes and </>/>> redirection",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Pipelines and redirection are real, not simulated: cmd1 | cmd2 | cmd3 forks",
                "every stage with its own pipe plumbing and one shared process group, so Ctrl-Z",
                "and job control (see \"help jobs\") apply to the whole pipeline, not just one",
                "stage of it.",
                "",
                "    program > file      write stdout to file, replacing its contents",
                "    program >> file     write stdout to file, appending to it",
                "    program < file      read stdin from file",
                "",
                "A pipeline's own exit status reflects its LAST stage, exactly like every other",
                "shell -- an earlier stage failing to be found is reported on its own line but",
                "doesn't change that overall status."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "jobs",
            "Aliases": ["job-control", "fg", "bg"],
            "Summary": "background jobs, Ctrl-Z suspend/resume, fg and bg",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Job control is real POSIX job control, not simulated: every job (a single",
                "command or a whole pipeline) gets its own real process group.",
                "",
                "    command &     starts it in the background and returns to the prompt",
                "                  right away, printing \"[N] pid\"",
                "    jobs          lists every background/stopped job and its current state",
                "    fg [%N]       brings a job to the foreground (the most recent one if no",
                "                  %N is given) and waits for it, exactly like typing the",
                "                  command directly",
                "    bg [%N]       resumes a STOPPED job in the background",
                "",
                "Ctrl-Z suspends whatever is currently in the foreground -- including a command",
                "typed directly, with no trailing & -- and prints \"[N]+  Stopped  ...\"; the",
                "shell itself is never interrupted or stopped by Ctrl-C/Ctrl-Z, only the job you",
                "are running."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "oops",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "retry the last command-not-found with a corrected name",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "oops <command> replaces only the EXECUTABLE NAME of the immediately preceding",
                "command, if (and only if) that command failed with \"command not found\" --",
                "everything else about it (arguments, redirection, pipeline structure, quoting)",
                "is preserved exactly, operating on the parsed command structure, never on its",
                "raw text. A quoted argument that happens to contain the failed name is left",
                "completely untouched.",
                "",
                "    gti status        (command not found: gti)",
                "    oops git          arcosh: retrying: git status  -- and runs it for real",
                "",
                "In a pipeline, the stage that actually failed to run is always the one fixed --",
                "never necessarily the first stage of the pipeline."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "history",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "the history built-in",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "history lists every command you've run this session, in order, alongside how",
                "it turned out (found and ran, command not found, etc.) -- the same structured",
                "record oops itself reads from. Classic-BASIC program-line editing (LIST/RUN/NEW",
                "-- see \"help program\") and ArcoBASIC immediate input are intentionally NOT",
                "recorded here; they're a different category of input (RFC-0052 section 8)."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "program",
            "Aliases": ["resident", "list"],
            "Summary": "the classic numbered-line BASIC program editor (LIST/RUN/NEW)",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Typing a line that starts with a number edits an in-memory resident program",
                "instead of running anything immediately -- classic BASIC line-editing rules:",
                "",
                "    10 PRINT \"ONE\"      inserts (or replaces) line 10",
                "    20                   an existing line number with nothing after it",
                "                         deletes that line",
                "",
                "Lines stay in numeric order regardless of the order you type them in.",
                "",
                "    LIST      prints the whole resident program back, in order",
                "    RUN       runs it (GOTO and line-number control flow work exactly like",
                "              classic BASIC); RUN <file> instead runs a script FILE on disk",
                "              (see \"help run\") and leaves the resident program untouched",
                "    NEW       clears the resident program"
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "immediate",
            "Aliases": [],
            "Summary": "typing plain ArcoBASIC statements directly at the prompt",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "A line typed at the prompt that looks like an ArcoBASIC statement -- one",
                "starting with a keyword such as PRINT/IF/FOR/WHILE/FUNCTION/RETURN, or a plain",
                "or compound assignment (x = 5, x += 1) -- runs immediately as ArcoBASIC instead",
                "of being treated as a command line:",
                "",
                "    PRINT \"hello\"      -> hello",
                "    x = 5",
                "    PRINT x            -> 5",
                "",
                "Variables persist across separate lines within one session (a real REPL, not a",
                "fresh interpreter every time) -- classic BASIC immediate-mode semantics. A",
                "single line is always a complete statement; multi-line constructs (a whole FOR",
                "loop, a FUNCTION body) belong in the resident program instead (\"help program\")."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "run",
            "Aliases": ["script"],
            "Summary": "RUN <file> -- executing a .abas script file",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "RUN <file> reads a real file from disk and runs it through the exact same",
                "compiler every other ArcoBASIC program goes through -- numbered lines,",
                "unnumbered structured code, and a file mixing both all just work, with no",
                "special-casing for classic line numbers. Accepts either a Linux path or an",
                "Arcology colon-path (see \"help paths\"). A missing file produces a clean error",
                "message rather than a crash.",
                "",
                "Bare RUN with no filename instead runs the resident numbered program you've",
                "typed in at the prompt -- see \"help program\"."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "themes",
            "Aliases": ["color", "colour", "display"],
            "Summary": "color output, themes, and how to disable it",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "ArcoSH styles its own prompt and messages (success/warning/error/etc.) using",
                "real ANSI color, automatically detecting what your terminal actually supports:",
                "24-bit truecolor, 256-color, plain 16-color, or no color at all. Detection uses",
                "the COLORTERM and TERM environment variables, and disabling color entirely with",
                "--no-color or the NO_COLOR environment variable is always respected first.",
                "",
                "Every message's own TEXT already carries its full meaning with color stripped",
                "out entirely -- color is a visual enhancement layered on top, never load-bearing",
                "for understanding what happened, so piping ArcoSH's output or using a plain",
                "terminal never loses any information, just the color.",
                "",
                "    theme                       shows the active theme and previews every role",
                "    theme edit                  interactive editor: pick a role, pick a color by",
                "                                NAME (red, blue, teal, ...), see it live -- no",
                "                                ANSI codes required",
                "    theme list                  lists built-in and saved themes",
                "    theme set <role> <tier> <code>   edits one role/tier directly by raw code",
                "    theme save <name>           saves the active theme under a name",
                "    theme use <name>            switches to a built-in or saved theme",
                "",
                "See \"help profiles\" for making a customized theme (and prompt) persist across",
                "sessions."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "prompt-customization",
            "Aliases": ["prompt"],
            "Summary": "composing the prompt from segments",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "The prompt is composed from named segments (RFC-0052 section 20), rendered in",
                "order and joined with spaces. The default is just one segment, \"classic\" --",
                "the original arcosh:<path> look -- so nobody who hasn't customized anything",
                "sees any change.",
                "",
                "    prompt                      shows the active segments/tags and a live preview",
                "    prompt edit                 interactive editor: add/remove/reorder segments",
                "                                and tag them with colors, one step at a time,",
                "                                with a live preview after each change",
                "    prompt set <seg1,seg2,...>   replaces the whole segment list in one go",
                "    prompt tag <segment> <role>  pins one segment to a fixed theme color (see",
                "                                \"color tags\" below); role \"default\" clears it",
                "",
                "Known segments:",
                "    classic     \"arcosh:\" followed by the current Arcology path (the default)",
                "    path        just the current Arcology path, no \"arcosh:\" prefix",
                "    hostname    this machine's hostname",
                "    user        the real OS username you're running as (styled \"error\" instead",
                "                of the usual \"prompt\" role by default when that's root -- the",
                "                classic red-prompt-as-root warning -- override with \"prompt tag",
                "                user <role>\" like any other segment)",
                "    exitcode    the last command's exit status (styled success/error)",
                "    jobs        the number of background/stopped jobs, hidden when there are",
                "                none",
                "    date        today's date (YYYY-MM-DD)",
                "    time        the current wall-clock time (HH:MM:SS)",
                "    year        4-digit year, year2   2-digit year",
                "    month       2-digit month (01-12), day     2-digit day of month (01-31)",
                "    hour        2-digit 24h hour, minute   2-digit minute, second   2-digit second",
                "    millisecond 3-digit millisecond (000-999) -- combine these individually",
                "                instead of \"date\"/\"time\" for a custom layout, e.g. \"prompt set",
                "                day,month,year2,hour,minute\" for a DD/MM/YY HH:MM style",
                "",
                "Color tags: every segment renders in its own default color (see \"help themes\"",
                "for the role names), but any segment can be pinned to a specific role instead --",
                "\"prompt tag hostname error\" makes the hostname segment always render in the",
                "active theme's alarming \"error\" color, regardless of what else is happening on",
                "screen. Useful for making a specific machine, or a specific piece of context,",
                "impossible to miss -- e.g. a sysadmin working across several boxes tagging",
                "\"hostname\" (or \"path\") with \"error\" or \"warning\" on the one production host",
                "they need to always notice they're on. \"exitcode\" can be tagged too, which",
                "replaces its own dynamic success/error coloring with the one fixed tagged color.",
                "",
                "See \"help profiles\" to make a custom segment list (and its tags) persist across",
                "sessions."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "profiles",
            "Aliases": ["profile", "config", "configuration"],
            "Summary": "saving your theme and prompt setup so it persists",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Theme and prompt changes (\"help themes\", \"help prompt-customization\") only",
                "last for the current session until saved. Settings live in ~/.arcosh (or",
                "$ARCOSH_HOME if set) as plain, human-readable \"Key: value\" text -- never",
                "executable ArcoBASIC code, by design (RFC-0052 section 22: configuration and",
                "arbitrary startup code must never be indistinguishable concepts).",
                "",
                "    profile                 shows what would be saved right now",
                "    profile save            saves the active theme, prompt segments/color tags,",
                "                            and color policy as the default for future sessions",
                "    profile save <name>     does that AND keeps a separate named snapshot you",
                "                            can return to later",
                "    profile load <name>     restores a named snapshot and makes it the default",
                "                            going forward",
                "    profile list            lists your saved named snapshots",
                "",
                "\"profile save\" captures whatever color policy is ACTIVE right now, including a",
                "one-off --no-color flag for this run -- running with --no-color once and then",
                "saving really does make future sessions start in no-color too, the same way",
                "saving current settings works in most tools.",
                "",
                "An executable ArcoBASIC startup file (for arbitrary automation, as opposed to",
                "these plain settings) is planned but not implemented yet."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "plugins",
            "Aliases": ["plugin"],
            "Summary": "installing/enabling real plugin .abas files that add commands/prompt segments/themes",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "Plugins are ordinary .abas files under ~/.arcosh/plugins (or $ARCOSH_HOME/plugins)",
                "that register real capabilities arcosh actually calls -- a new command, a new",
                "prompt segment, a new theme -- not just static text.",
                "",
                "    plugins                     lists installed plugins, enabled/disabled, any",
                "                                load failures, and every capability each loaded",
                "                                plugin has registered",
                "    plugins install <path> [name]   copies a .abas file in as a named plugin",
                "    plugins enable <name>        activates it (now, and on every future start)",
                "    plugins disable <name>       deactivates it for future starts",
                "",
                "A plugin registers capabilities with four functions, available automatically",
                "inside every plugin's own source (no import needed):",
                "",
                "    Shell.RegisterCommand(name, ADDRESSOF MyFunction)",
                "    Shell.RegisterPromptSegment(name, ADDRESSOF MyFunction)",
                "    Shell.RegisterTheme(name, {\"error\": {\"TrueColor\": \"...\", ...}, ...})",
                "    Shell.RegisterCompleter(name, ADDRESSOF MyFunction)",
                "",
                "A registered command becomes a real shell built-in (\"help commands\") -- it takes",
                "precedence over a same-named real program on PATH, and never sees pipes,",
                "redirection, or backgrounding, only its own arguments. A registered prompt segment",
                "works exactly like a built-in one in \"prompt set\"/\"prompt edit\" (\"help",
                "prompt-customization\"). A registered theme can be selected with \"theme use",
                "<name>\" exactly like a saved one, filled in with DefaultTheme's own values for",
                "any role/tier it doesn't specify.",
                "",
                "Completer capabilities can be registered and listed today, but nothing in arcosh",
                "yet CALLS one -- there is no tab-completion system at all yet (a separate, later",
                "work package). Disclosed here rather than pretending it does something.",
                "",
                "A broken plugin (fails to load, or throws when a registered command/segment runs)",
                "is reported clearly and never crashes the shell or blocks other plugins from",
                "loading. Start with \"arcosh --no-plugins\" to skip loading plugins entirely if",
                "one is causing real trouble."
            ], "\n")
        },
        {
            "Name": "arcobasic",
            "Aliases": ["basic", "syntax", "language"],
            "Summary": "a short introduction to ArcoBASIC syntax for scripting",
            "Body": String.Join([
                "This is a short introduction, not the full ArcoBASIC language reference --",
                "there's much more (classes, TRY/CATCH, tuples, bit vectors, comprehensions,",
                "and more) that a fuller in-shell reference will eventually cover.",
                "",
                "    x = 5                       assignment (no LET required)",
                "    PRINT \"x is \" + STRING(x)    string concatenation with +",
                "    IF x > 3 THEN",
                "        PRINT \"big\"",
                "    ELSE",
                "        PRINT \"small\"",
                "    END IF",
                "    FOR i = 1 TO 5",
                "        PRINT i",
                "    NEXT",
                "    WHILE x > 0",
                "        x = x - 1",
                "    WEND",
                "    FUNCTION Double(n)",
                "        RETURN n * 2",
                "    END FUNCTION",
                "",
                "Arrays: [1, 2, 3]  --  Objects: {\"Name\": \"Ada\"}  --  both support Foo.Bar-style",
                "field access and indexing. See the project's own docs/ directory for the",
                "complete language documentation until a fuller in-shell reference exists."
            ], "\n")
        }
    ]
END FUNCTION

' Case-insensitive lookup by Name or any Aliases entry. Returns an object with an "IsNothing"
' marker (an ordinary comparison via TYPEOF works fine on the real topic objects returned by
' HelpTopics, but ArcoBASIC has no single canonical "not found" sentinel that's simpler than just
' returning one explicitly here) rather than the language's own "nothing"/null, only because a
' single explicit shape is easier for RunHelpCommand/the selftest to check without a special case.
FUNCTION FindHelpTopic(topics AS ARRAY, query AS STRING)
    needle = Upper(query)
    FOR topic IN topics
        IF Upper(topic.Name) == needle THEN RETURN topic
        FOR alias IN topic.Aliases
            IF Upper(alias) == needle THEN RETURN topic
        NEXT
    NEXT
    RETURN {"IsNothing": TRUE}
END FUNCTION

' `help` / `?` [topic]. Bare form lists every topic with its summary and any alias; with an
' argument, shows that one topic's full body. Styled (prompt role for topic names, error role for
' an unknown topic) through the same Display.* functions everything else in this file uses --
' never a raw PRINT with an ANSI code inline.
FUNCTION RunHelpCommand(arguments AS ARRAY)
    topics = HelpTopics()

    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN
        ignored = Display.Style("prompt")
        ignored = Display.Line("ArcoSH help topics (type: help <topic>)")
        ignored = Display.Reset()
        FOR topic IN topics
            line = "  " + topic.Name
            IF LEN(topic.Aliases) > 0 THEN line = line + " (" + String.Join(topic.Aliases, ", ") + ")"
            ' At least one space always -- a name+alias list longer than targetColumn (e.g.
            ' "pipelines (redirection)") must not run directly into the summary with no
            ' separator at all.
            padding = " "
            targetColumn = 22
            WHILE LEN(line) + LEN(padding) < targetColumn
                padding = padding + " "
            WEND
            PRINT line + padding + topic.Summary
        NEXT
        RETURN
    END IF

    found = FindHelpTopic(topics, arguments[0])
    IF Object.Has(found, "IsNothing") THEN
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: help: no such topic: " + arguments[0] + " (try bare \"help\" for the topic list)")
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = Display.Style("prompt")
    ignored = Display.Line(found.Name)
    ignored = Display.Reset()
    PRINT found.Body
END FUNCTION

' Structural regression coverage -- topic-table integrity (every topic has real content, lookup
' by name and by alias both work, an unknown topic is reported cleanly) rather than exact printed
' formatting, which is cosmetic and not worth pinning down byte-for-byte.
FUNCTION RunHelpSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    topics = HelpTopics()

    IF LEN(topics) > 0 THEN
        PRINT "ok   HelpTopics returns at least one topic (" + STRING(LEN(topics)) + ")"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL HelpTopics returned no topics at all"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    FOR topic IN topics
        hasContent = LEN(topic.Name) > 0
        IF hasContent THEN hasContent = LEN(topic.Summary) > 0
        IF hasContent THEN hasContent = LEN(topic.Body) > 0
        IF hasContent THEN
            PRINT "ok   topic " + topic.Name + " has a name, summary, and body"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL topic " + topic.Name + " is missing content"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    NEXT

    byName = FindHelpTopic(topics, "Jobs")
    IF Object.Has(byName, "IsNothing") THEN
        PRINT "FAIL lookup by name (case-insensitive) failed"
        failures = failures + 1
    ELSE
        PRINT "ok   lookup by name (case-insensitive) found: " + byName.Name
    END IF

    byAlias = FindHelpTopic(topics, "fg")
    IF Object.Has(byAlias, "IsNothing") THEN
        PRINT "FAIL lookup by alias failed"
        failures = failures + 1
    ELSE
        IF byAlias.Name == "jobs" THEN
            PRINT "ok   lookup by alias resolved to the right topic: " + byAlias.Name
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL lookup by alias resolved to the wrong topic: " + byAlias.Name
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    END IF

    missing = FindHelpTopic(topics, "not-a-real-topic")
    IF Object.Has(missing, "IsNothing") THEN
        PRINT "ok   an unknown topic reports not found instead of crashing"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL an unknown topic did not report not found"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Profiles: Configuration, Themes, and Prompt Customization (arcosh built-in -- not one of
' RFC-0052's own numbered work packages; implements section 20, "Prompt Architecture" -- "The
' prompt SHOULD be composed from segments" -- and section 22, "Configuration" -- "Configuration
' SHOULD primarily be declarative... Configuration and arbitrary startup code MUST NOT be
' indistinguishable concepts"). The on-disk format for both config and themes is deliberately a
' tiny, hand-rollable "Key: value" line format (ParseDeclarativeLines below), not ArcoBASIC
' source text run through Runtime.RunString/EvalImmediate -- running arbitrary ArcoBASIC to load
' "configuration" would make config and startup code exactly the indistinguishable concept this
' section explicitly forbids. An executable startup file (RFC section 22's own "explicitly
' executable ArcoBASIC startup file") is real, disclosed, unattempted future work -- config here
' only ever describes VALUES (active theme name, prompt segments, color policy), never behavior.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ~/.arcosh by default, or $ARCOSH_HOME if set -- the same convention the retired pre-RFC-0052
' arco_shell already used (docs/arcosh.md), reused here rather than inventing a new one.
FUNCTION ProfileHome()
    override = Process.Env("ARCOSH_HOME")
    IF override <> "" THEN RETURN override
    RETURN Process.Env("HOME") + "/.arcosh"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ThemesDir()
    RETURN ProfileHome() + "/themes"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ProfilesDir()
    RETURN ProfileHome() + "/profiles"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ConfigPath()
    RETURN ProfileHome() + "/config"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ThemePath(name AS STRING)
    RETURN ThemesDir() + "/" + name
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ProfilePath(name AS STRING)
    RETURN ProfilesDir() + "/" + name
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION PluginsDir()
    RETURN ProfileHome() + "/plugins"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION PluginsEnabledPath()
    RETURN PluginsDir() + "/enabled.txt"
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION PluginFilePath(name AS STRING)
    RETURN PluginsDir() + "/" + name + ".abas"
END FUNCTION

' Created lazily, only when something is actually about to be written -- a plain interactive
' session that never touches theme/prompt/profile commands never creates ~/.arcosh at all,
' matching how the rest of this file only ever does real filesystem work in response to a real
' command, never speculatively at startup.
FUNCTION EnsureProfileDirectories()
    ignored = Directory.Create(ThemesDir())
    ignored = Directory.Create(ProfilesDir())
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION EnsurePluginsDirectory()
    ignored = Directory.Create(PluginsDir())
END FUNCTION

' Shared by both config and theme files: trims each line, skips blank lines and "#" comments,
' splits on the FIRST ":" (a color CODE's own value can itself contain ";" but never a bare ":",
' so this never needs to worry about a value containing the delimiter). Genuinely just data --
' no expression evaluation, no statements, nothing Runtime.RunString would need to run.
FUNCTION ParseDeclarativeLines(text AS STRING)
    result = []
    FOR rawLine IN String.Split(text, "\n")
        line = String.Trim(rawLine)
        IF line == "" THEN CONTINUE FOR
        IF String.Slice(line, 0, 1) == "#" THEN CONTINUE FOR
        colonIndex = -1
        i = 0
        WHILE i < LEN(line)
            IF String.Slice(line, i, 1) == ":" THEN
                colonIndex = i
                EXIT WHILE
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        IF colonIndex < 0 THEN CONTINUE FOR
        key = String.Trim(String.Slice(line, 0, colonIndex))
        value = String.Trim(String.Slice(line, colonIndex + 1, LEN(line)))
        ignored = Array.Push(result, {"Key": key, "Value": value})
    NEXT
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION FindDeclarativeValue(pairs AS ARRAY, key AS STRING)
    needle = Upper(key)
    FOR pair IN pairs
        IF Upper(pair.Key) == needle THEN RETURN pair.Value
    NEXT
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

' Fixed, stable order for serialization -- matches DefaultTheme's own field order, purely so a
' saved theme file reads the same way twice in a row instead of shuffling by hash-map iteration
' order.
FUNCTION ThemeRoleOrder()
    RETURN ["normal", "prompt", "path", "success", "warning", "error", "muted", "selection"]
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ThemeTierOrder()
    RETURN ["TrueColor", "Color256", "Color16"]
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerializeTheme(theme AS OBJECT)
    lines = []
    FOR roleName IN ThemeRoleOrder()
        roleEntry = Object.Get(theme, roleName)
        parts = []
        FOR tierName IN ThemeTierOrder()
            code = Object.Get(roleEntry, tierName)
            ignored = Array.Push(parts, tierName + "=" + code)
        NEXT
        ignored = Array.Push(lines, roleName + ": " + String.Join(parts, ", "))
    NEXT
    RETURN String.Join(lines, "\n")
END FUNCTION

' A partial theme file (missing roles, or a role missing a tier) is filled in from DefaultTheme
' for whatever it doesn't specify -- every role/tier this backend's StyleCode ever looks up is
' always present in the result, so a hand-edited or half-finished theme file degrades to the
' built-in color for anything it doesn't override rather than rendering with no style at all.
FUNCTION ParseThemeText(text AS STRING)
    base = DefaultTheme()
    pairs = ParseDeclarativeLines(text)
    result = {}
    FOR roleName IN ThemeRoleOrder()
        baseRole = Object.Get(base, roleName)
        rawValue = FindDeclarativeValue(pairs, roleName)
        roleResult = {"TrueColor": Object.Get(baseRole, "TrueColor"),
            "Color256": Object.Get(baseRole, "Color256"), "Color16": Object.Get(baseRole, "Color16")}
        IF rawValue <> "" THEN
            FOR spec IN String.Split(rawValue, ",")
                trimmedSpec = String.Trim(spec)
                equalsIndex = -1
                i = 0
                WHILE i < LEN(trimmedSpec)
                    IF String.Slice(trimmedSpec, i, 1) == "=" THEN
                        equalsIndex = i
                        EXIT WHILE
                    END IF
                    i = i + 1
                WEND
                IF equalsIndex >= 0 THEN
                    tierName = String.Trim(String.Slice(trimmedSpec, 0, equalsIndex))
                    tierValue = String.Trim(String.Slice(trimmedSpec, equalsIndex + 1, LEN(trimmedSpec)))
                    ' Object.Set returns a MODIFIED COPY, unlike Array.Push -- it never mutates
                    ' its first argument in place, so the result must always be reassigned.
                    roleResult = Object.Set(roleResult, tierName, tierValue)
                END IF
            NEXT
        END IF
        result = Object.Set(result, roleName, roleResult)
    NEXT
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

' "default" is always the built-in DefaultTheme(), never a file on disk -- a saved theme
' literally named "default" would shadow nothing, since this checks the name before ever
' touching the filesystem. A named theme that doesn't exist on disk gracefully falls back to
' DefaultTheme() too (a typo in "theme use" is reported by RunThemeCommand itself, which checks
' existence separately -- this loader's own job is just "give me a usable theme object no matter
' what", never failing outright).
' Fills in DefaultTheme()'s own values for any role/tier a PARTIAL theme object doesn't supply --
' the same defaulting ParseThemeText already does for a theme FILE's text, factored out so a
' plugin-registered "theme" capability (RFC-0052 WP-009, a raw OBJECT, not file text) gets the
' identical guarantee: every role/tier StyleCode ever looks up is always present.
FUNCTION FillThemeDefaults(partial AS OBJECT)
    base = DefaultTheme()
    result = {}
    FOR roleName IN ThemeRoleOrder()
        baseRole = Object.Get(base, roleName)
        suppliedRole = Object.Get(partial, roleName, {})
        roleResult = {}
        FOR tierName IN ThemeTierOrder()
            tierValue = Object.Get(suppliedRole, tierName, "")
            IF tierValue == "" THEN tierValue = Object.Get(baseRole, tierName)
            roleResult = Object.Set(roleResult, tierName, tierValue)
        NEXT
        result = Object.Set(result, roleName, roleResult)
    NEXT
    RETURN result
END FUNCTION

' "default" is always the built-in DefaultTheme(); a name saved to disk (theme save/theme edit's
' own "s") wins next; a plugin-registered "theme" capability (RFC-0052 WP-009) is the last
' fallback before giving up and using DefaultTheme() anyway -- a typo in "theme use" is reported by
' RunThemeCommand itself, which checks existence/registration separately.
FUNCTION LoadTheme(name AS STRING)
    IF name == "default" THEN RETURN DefaultTheme()
    path = ThemePath(name)
    IF File.Exists(path) THEN RETURN ParseThemeText(File.ReadText(path))
    IF Shell.PluginHasCapability("theme", name) THEN RETURN FillThemeDefaults(Shell.PluginCapabilityValue("theme", name))
    RETURN DefaultTheme()
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SaveTheme(name AS STRING, theme AS OBJECT)
    ignored = EnsureProfileDirectories()
    ignored = File.WriteText(ThemePath(name), SerializeTheme(theme))
END FUNCTION

' Session-active theme CONTENT (an object) and NAME (a string, purely for display/config-saving
' purposes -- "theme set" edits the content in place without renaming it; only "theme
' save"/"theme use" change which name is considered active) -- both held via Runtime.GetGlobal/
' SetGlobal for the same reason CurrentColorTier's own comment gives: a function far away from
' the main script body (StyleCode, called from every Display.* call) needs to read this.
FUNCTION ActiveTheme()
    theme = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_active_theme")
    IF TYPEOF(theme) <> "Object" THEN RETURN DefaultTheme()
    RETURN theme
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ActiveThemeName()
    name = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_active_theme_name")
    IF name == "" THEN RETURN "default"
    RETURN name
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SetActiveTheme(name AS STRING, theme AS OBJECT)
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_active_theme_name", name)
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_active_theme", theme)
END FUNCTION

' Known, renderable prompt segment names -- RFC section 20's own suggested built-ins (hostname,
' current Arcology path, previous exit status, background job count) plus "classic", the
' original hardcoded "arcosh:<path>" look kept as its own segment so the DEFAULT segment list can
' reproduce it exactly (see Prompt's own comment) rather than every existing session's prompt
' changing shape just because segments now exist as a concept, plus "date"/"time" (today's date,
' the current wall-clock time) built on Time.Now()'s own fixed "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" format.
' Includes every currently-registered "prompt.segment" plugin capability (RFC-0052 WP-009) on top
' of the built-ins, so a plugin segment is accepted by "prompt set"/"prompt edit"'s own
' Array.Contains validation exactly like a real built-in -- a plugin that hasn't loaded (or failed
' to load) simply never adds its name here, matching Shell.PluginCapabilities' own "only what's
' actually registered right now" contract.
FUNCTION KnownPromptSegments()
    names = ["classic", "path", "hostname", "user", "exitcode", "jobs", "date", "time",
        "year", "year2", "month", "day", "hour", "minute", "second", "millisecond"]
    FOR cap IN Shell.PluginCapabilities()
        IF cap.Capability == "prompt.segment" THEN ignored = Array.Push(names, cap.Name)
    NEXT
    RETURN names
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION DefaultPromptSegments()
    RETURN ["classic"]
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ActivePromptSegments()
    segments = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_prompt_segments")
    IF TYPEOF(segments) <> "Array" THEN RETURN DefaultPromptSegments()
    IF LEN(segments) == 0 THEN RETURN DefaultPromptSegments()
    RETURN segments
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SetActivePromptSegments(segments AS ARRAY)
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_prompt_segments", segments)
END FUNCTION

' Each active segment's own BUILT-IN role -- what it renders as when nothing has explicitly
' overridden it (see ActivePromptTags/ResolveSegmentRole just below for the override itself).
' "exitcode" has no fixed entry here -- ResolveSegmentRole special-cases it, since its whole point
' is switching between "success" and "error" based on the actual last exit code, not one constant
' role -- but it too can be pinned to one fixed role like any other segment by tagging it.
FUNCTION DefaultSegmentRole(name AS STRING)
    IF name == "classic" THEN RETURN "path"
    IF name == "path" THEN RETURN "path"
    IF name == "hostname" THEN RETURN "prompt"
    IF name == "jobs" THEN RETURN "warning"
    dateTimeSegments = ["date", "time", "year", "year2", "month", "day", "hour", "minute", "second", "millisecond"]
    IF Array.Contains(dateTimeSegments, name) THEN RETURN "muted"
    RETURN "normal"
END FUNCTION

' Per-segment color TAGS (RFC-0052 section 20's own segment model, extended on request): a segment
' normally renders in its own DefaultSegmentRole, but any active theme role can be pinned onto any
' segment instead -- e.g. `prompt tag hostname error` makes the hostname segment always render in
' the theme's "error" color, unmistakable regardless of what else is happening on screen, the kind
' of always-visible warning color a sysadmin working across several machines (or one sensitive
' production host) wants for the segment that identifies WHICH machine this is. Stored as a plain
' {segmentName: roleName} object, held via Runtime.GetGlobal/SetGlobal for the same reason every
' other session-wide piece of state in this file is (see CurrentColorTier's own comment) -- a
' segment with no entry (Object.Get's own "" for a missing key) simply has no override.
FUNCTION ActivePromptTags()
    tags = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_prompt_tags")
    IF TYPEOF(tags) <> "Object" THEN RETURN {}
    RETURN tags
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SetActivePromptTags(tags AS OBJECT)
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_prompt_tags", tags)
END FUNCTION

' Pure (no globals, no I/O) -- see RunEditorSelfTest. "default" as the role name clears an
' existing tag (a real theme role literally named "default" doesn't exist -- ThemeRoleOrder never
' contains it -- so this can never collide with a genuine role).
FUNCTION TagPromptSegment(tags AS OBJECT, segment AS STRING, role AS STRING)
    IF !Array.Contains(KnownPromptSegments(), segment) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Tags": tags, "Error": "unknown segment: " + segment}
    END IF
    IF role <> "default" ANDALSO !Array.Contains(ThemeRoleOrder(), role) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Tags": tags,
            "Error": "unknown role: " + role + " (known: " + String.Join(ThemeRoleOrder(), ", ") + ", or \"default\" to clear a tag)"}
    END IF
    tags = Object.Set(tags, segment, role)
    RETURN {"Ok": TRUE, "Tags": tags, "Error": ""}
END FUNCTION

' Bundles everything a segment could possibly need to render itself into ONE object, rather than
' growing RenderPromptSegment/ResolveSegmentRole's own parameter list every time a new segment
' needs one more piece of state -- which is exactly what adding "user" just did: 8 plain
' parameters (7 pointer-ish, 1 number) tipped RenderPromptSegment over this native backend's
' System V fast path limit of 6 integer/pointer argument registers ("call ... passes more
' integer/pointer arguments than this backend's System V fast path supports"), a real compile
' failure caught immediately on rebuild. A single OBJECT parameter is one pointer no matter how
' many fields it carries, so this both fixes that and gives WP-009's eventual
' `Shell.RegisterPromptSegment` a natural one-argument shape to hand a plugin's own render
' callback, rather than an ever-growing positional list.
FUNCTION MakePromptContext(cwd AS STRING, lastExitCode AS NUMBER, jobsTable AS ARRAY, tier AS STRING, resetCode AS STRING, tags AS OBJECT, currentUser AS STRING)
    RETURN {"Cwd": cwd, "LastExitCode": lastExitCode, "JobsTable": jobsTable, "Tier": tier,
        "ResetCode": resetCode, "Tags": tags, "CurrentUser": currentUser}
END FUNCTION

' The role a segment ACTUALLY renders in right now: an explicit tag always wins; "exitcode" with
' no tag keeps its own dynamic success/error behavior; anything else with no tag falls back to
' DefaultSegmentRole.
FUNCTION ResolveSegmentRole(context AS OBJECT, name AS STRING)
    ' Object.Get's own 2-argument form returns a genuine NULL for a missing key, not "" -- the
    ' explicit 3-argument default form is required here so an untagged segment (the ordinary case)
    ' is correctly treated as "no tag" rather than as if it were tagged with a null value.
    tag = Object.Get(context.Tags, name, "")
    IF tag <> "" ANDALSO tag <> "default" THEN RETURN tag
    IF name == "exitcode" THEN
        IF context.LastExitCode <> 0 THEN RETURN "error"
        RETURN "success"
    END IF
    ' "user" defaults to the alarming "error" color specifically when running as root -- the
    ' classic "your prompt turns red as a warning you're root" convention many other shells use,
    ' the exact "make certain parts easier to see on the fly for sysadmin roles" ask this whole
    ' color-tag mechanism exists for, given for free as a sensible default rather than something
    ' that has to be tagged manually. Still fully overridable with an explicit "prompt tag user
    ' <role>" like any other segment.
    IF name == "user" THEN
        IF context.CurrentUser == "root" THEN RETURN "error"
        RETURN "prompt"
    END IF
    RETURN DefaultSegmentRole(name)
END FUNCTION

' One self-contained styled fragment per segment (its own style AND reset), so segments render
' correctly regardless of how many others are joined around them. "" for a segment that has
' nothing to show right now (jobs, with none running) or an unrecognized name -- Prompt's own
' caller already drops empty segments rather than leaving a stray extra space.
FUNCTION RenderPromptSegment(name AS STRING, context AS OBJECT)
    tier = context.Tier
    resetCode = context.ResetCode
    style = StyleCode(ResolveSegmentRole(context, name), tier)
    IF name == "classic" THEN RETURN "arcosh:" + resetCode + style + Path.ToArcology(context.Cwd)
    IF name == "path" THEN RETURN style + Path.ToArcology(context.Cwd) + resetCode
    IF name == "hostname" THEN RETURN style + Host.Hostname() + resetCode
    IF name == "user" THEN RETURN style + context.CurrentUser + resetCode
    IF name == "exitcode" THEN RETURN style + STRING(context.LastExitCode) + resetCode
    IF name == "jobs" THEN
        count = LEN(context.JobsTable)
        IF count == 0 THEN RETURN ""
        RETURN style + STRING(count) + "j" + resetCode
    END IF
    ' Time.Now()'s own fixed format is "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" (19 characters, every field already
    ' zero-padded by std::put_time) -- date/time and each individual field below are all sliced
    ' directly out of it rather than adding a dedicated primitive per field. Each segment fetches
    ' its own fresh Time.Now() (matching a real, disclosed limitation "date"/"time" already had):
    ' combining several of these in one prompt (e.g. "year,month,day") can, in the rarest possible
    ' case, straddle a rollover between two calls (year 23:59:59.999 -> 00:00:00.000) and show a
    ' date one instant apart from the time -- never wrong once printed, just not perfectly atomic.
    IF name == "date" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 0, 10) + resetCode
    IF name == "time" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 11, 8) + resetCode
    IF name == "year" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 0, 4) + resetCode
    IF name == "year2" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 2, 2) + resetCode
    IF name == "month" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 5, 2) + resetCode
    IF name == "day" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 8, 2) + resetCode
    IF name == "hour" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 11, 2) + resetCode
    IF name == "minute" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 14, 2) + resetCode
    IF name == "second" THEN RETURN style + String.Slice(Time.Now(), 17, 2) + resetCode
    ' The only field NOT available from Time.Now()'s own string -- std::time_t's resolution is
    ' whole seconds, so this is Time.Milliseconds()'s own dedicated primitive instead, padded to
    ' a fixed 3 digits (STRING(7) is "7", not "007") the same way every other field above already
    ' is by std::put_time.
    IF name == "millisecond" THEN RETURN style + PadNumber(Time.Milliseconds(), 3) + resetCode
    ' Nothing built-in matched -- fall back to a plugin-registered "prompt.segment" capability
    ' (RFC-0052 WP-009). A plugin segment that throws when INVOKED (not just when loaded) renders
    ' as nothing rather than breaking the whole prompt, the same section 19.3 "a broken optional
    ' plugin SHOULD NOT make the shell unrecoverable" guarantee LoadEnabledPlugins already gives
    ' load failures.
    IF Shell.PluginHasCapability("prompt.segment", name) THEN
        TRY
            RETURN style + Shell.PluginInvoke("prompt.segment", name, context) + resetCode
        CATCH err
            RETURN ""
        END TRY
    END IF
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerializePromptTags(tags AS OBJECT)
    parts = []
    FOR segmentName IN KnownPromptSegments()
        tag = Object.Get(tags, segmentName, "")
        IF tag <> "" ANDALSO tag <> "default" THEN ignored = Array.Push(parts, segmentName + "=" + tag)
    NEXT
    RETURN String.Join(parts, ", ")
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ParsePromptTagsText(text AS STRING)
    tags = {}
    IF text == "" THEN RETURN tags
    FOR spec IN String.Split(text, ",")
        trimmedSpec = String.Trim(spec)
        IF trimmedSpec == "" THEN CONTINUE FOR
        equalsIndex = -1
        i = 0
        WHILE i < LEN(trimmedSpec)
            IF String.Slice(trimmedSpec, i, 1) == "=" THEN
                equalsIndex = i
                EXIT WHILE
            END IF
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        IF equalsIndex < 0 THEN CONTINUE FOR
        segmentName = String.Trim(String.Slice(trimmedSpec, 0, equalsIndex))
        roleName = String.Trim(String.Slice(trimmedSpec, equalsIndex + 1, LEN(trimmedSpec)))
        tags = Object.Set(tags, segmentName, roleName)
    NEXT
    RETURN tags
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerializeConfig(config AS OBJECT)
    lines = ["Theme: " + config.Theme]
    ignored = Array.Push(lines, "PromptSegments: " + String.Join(config.PromptSegments, ", "))
    ignored = Array.Push(lines, "PromptTags: " + SerializePromptTags(config.PromptTags))
    ignored = Array.Push(lines, "NoColor: " + STRING(config.NoColor))
    RETURN String.Join(lines, "\n")
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION ParseConfigText(text AS STRING)
    pairs = ParseDeclarativeLines(text)
    theme = FindDeclarativeValue(pairs, "Theme")
    IF theme == "" THEN theme = "default"

    segmentsRaw = FindDeclarativeValue(pairs, "PromptSegments")
    segments = DefaultPromptSegments()
    IF segmentsRaw <> "" THEN
        segments = []
        FOR seg IN String.Split(segmentsRaw, ",")
            ignored = Array.Push(segments, String.Trim(seg))
        NEXT
    END IF

    tags = ParsePromptTagsText(FindDeclarativeValue(pairs, "PromptTags"))

    noColor = FALSE
    IF Upper(FindDeclarativeValue(pairs, "NoColor")) == "TRUE" THEN noColor = TRUE

    RETURN {"Theme": theme, "PromptSegments": segments, "PromptTags": tags, "NoColor": noColor}
END FUNCTION

' Never fails -- a missing config file (the ordinary first-run case) is just the defaults, not
' an error condition worth reporting.
FUNCTION LoadConfig()
    path = ConfigPath()
    IF !File.Exists(path) THEN RETURN {"Theme": "default", "PromptSegments": DefaultPromptSegments(), "PromptTags": {}, "NoColor": FALSE}
    RETURN ParseConfigText(File.ReadText(path))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SaveConfig(config AS OBJECT)
    ignored = EnsureProfileDirectories()
    ignored = File.WriteText(ConfigPath(), SerializeConfig(config))
END FUNCTION

' Called once at startup (see the main script body below), after color-tier detection has already
' run: loads ~/.arcosh/config if present and applies its theme/prompt-segments/no-color settings
' to the session. --no-color and NO_COLOR are checked SEPARATELY at startup and always take
' precedence over a persisted "NoColor: true" in config -- an explicit flag/environment override
' beats a saved preference, the ordinary Unix convention -- so this only ever WIDENS the no-color
' decision (config saying yes when the flag/env didn't), never narrows an explicit no-color back
' to color.
FUNCTION ApplyStartupConfig()
    config = LoadConfig()
    ignored = SetActiveTheme(config.Theme, LoadTheme(config.Theme))
    ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(config.PromptSegments)
    ignored = SetActivePromptTags(config.PromptTags)
    RETURN config.NoColor
END FUNCTION

' Small general-purpose helper (also used by the theme/prompt editors below): true only if every
' character is an ASCII digit, so a menu number can be safely handed to NUMBER() -- NUMBER()
' itself calls the C++ std::stod under the hood (src/runtime/runtime.cpp), which throws on
' anything that isn't a valid numeral, so this must be checked FIRST rather than caught after.
FUNCTION IsAllDigits(text AS STRING)
    IF text == "" THEN RETURN FALSE
    i = 0
    WHILE i < LEN(text)
        IF !String.Contains("0123456789", String.Slice(text, i, 1)) THEN RETURN FALSE
        i = i + 1
    WEND
    RETURN TRUE
END FUNCTION

' Zero-pads a non-negative whole number out to `width` digits (STRING(7) is "7", not "007") --
' used by the "millisecond" prompt segment, the one date/time field not already zero-padded by
' Time.Now()'s own std::put_time formatting.
FUNCTION PadNumber(value AS NUMBER, width AS NUMBER)
    text = STRING(value)
    WHILE LEN(text) < width
        text = "0" + text
    WEND
    RETURN text
END FUNCTION

' A small curated palette of NAMED colors (not raw ANSI codes) so "design your look and feel" is
' "pick red" rather than "know that TrueColor red is 38;2;224;108;117 and Color256 red is
' 38;5;167 and Color16 red is 31" -- the whole reason a from-scratch "theme set" (still available,
' unchanged, for scripting/automation) never really counted as an EDITOR. Several of these
' deliberately reuse the exact codes DefaultTheme already uses for its own roles (red/error,
' green/success, yellow/warning, cyan/path, blue/prompt, gray/muted) so "pick red for a role"
' looks exactly as good as the bundled theme's own red.
FUNCTION NamedColorPalette()
    RETURN [
        {"Name": "red",     "TrueColor": "38;2;224;108;117", "Color256": "38;5;167", "Color16": "31"},
        {"Name": "green",   "TrueColor": "38;2;98;209;150",  "Color256": "38;5;71",  "Color16": "32"},
        {"Name": "yellow",  "TrueColor": "38;2;229;192;123", "Color256": "38;5;179", "Color16": "33"},
        {"Name": "blue",    "TrueColor": "38;2;97;175;239",  "Color256": "38;5;75",  "Color16": "34"},
        {"Name": "magenta", "TrueColor": "38;2;198;120;221", "Color256": "38;5;170", "Color16": "35"},
        {"Name": "cyan",    "TrueColor": "38;2;86;182;194",  "Color256": "38;5;73",  "Color16": "36"},
        {"Name": "white",   "TrueColor": "38;2;220;223;228", "Color256": "38;5;188", "Color16": "37"},
        {"Name": "gray",    "TrueColor": "2;38;2;150;150;150", "Color256": "2;38;5;244", "Color16": "2"},
        {"Name": "orange",  "TrueColor": "38;2;209;154;102", "Color256": "38;5;173", "Color16": "33"},
        {"Name": "purple",  "TrueColor": "38;2;171;142;255", "Color256": "38;5;141", "Color16": "35"},
        {"Name": "pink",    "TrueColor": "38;2;255;135;170", "Color256": "38;5;211", "Color16": "35"},
        {"Name": "teal",    "TrueColor": "38;2;86;194;173",  "Color256": "38;5;80",  "Color16": "36"}
    ]
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION NamedColorNames()
    names = []
    FOR entry IN NamedColorPalette()
        ignored = Array.Push(names, entry.Name)
    NEXT
    RETURN names
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION FindNamedColor(name AS STRING)
    needle = Lower(name)
    FOR entry IN NamedColorPalette()
        IF Lower(entry.Name) == needle THEN RETURN entry
    NEXT
    RETURN {"IsNothing": TRUE}
END FUNCTION

' Pure (no globals touched, no I/O) so it can be unit-tested directly -- see RunEditorSelfTest --
' independent of the interactive loop that calls it. Unknown color name: {"Ok": FALSE}, theme
' unchanged. "bold" prefixes "1;" onto all three tiers' codes, the same convention DefaultTheme's
' own "error" role already uses for its bold-red.
FUNCTION ApplyNamedColorToRole(theme AS OBJECT, role AS STRING, colorName AS STRING, bold AS BOOL)
    color = FindNamedColor(colorName)
    IF Object.Has(color, "IsNothing") THEN RETURN {"Theme": theme, "Ok": FALSE}
    prefix = ""
    IF bold THEN prefix = "1;"
    roleEntry = {"TrueColor": prefix + color.TrueColor, "Color256": prefix + color.Color256, "Color16": prefix + color.Color16}
    theme = Object.Set(theme, role, roleEntry)
    RETURN {"Theme": theme, "Ok": TRUE}
END FUNCTION

' Accepts either a 1-based menu number (as printed by RunThemeEditor) or the role's own name,
' case-insensitively -- "" for anything that resolves to neither, which every caller treats as
' "not a valid selection" rather than guessing.
FUNCTION ResolveRoleSelection(input AS STRING, roles AS ARRAY)
    trimmed = String.Trim(input)
    IF trimmed == "" THEN RETURN ""
    IF IsAllDigits(trimmed) THEN
        index = NUMBER(trimmed) - 1
        IF index >= 0 ANDALSO index < LEN(roles) THEN RETURN roles[index]
        RETURN ""
    END IF
    needle = Lower(trimmed)
    FOR role IN roles
        IF Lower(role) == needle THEN RETURN role
    NEXT
    RETURN ""
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION PrintThemePreview()
    ignored = Display.Style("prompt")
    ignored = Display.Line("active theme: " + ActiveThemeName())
    ignored = Display.Reset()
    FOR roleName IN ThemeRoleOrder()
        ignored = Display.Style(roleName)
        ignored = Display.Line("  " + roleName)
        ignored = Display.Reset()
    NEXT
END FUNCTION

' The per-role sub-editor `theme edit` drops into once a role is picked -- loops until the user
' backs out ("b") or hits EOF (Ctrl-D), which is treated as "stop editing" everywhere in this file
' Console.ReadLine is used, never as an error. Every change here is applied to the ACTIVE theme
' immediately (SetActiveTheme, live -- the same "theme set" already does), so the top-level
' editor's own preview reflects it right away; nothing is written to disk until "theme edit"'s own
' save step (or a separate "theme save"/"profile save").
FUNCTION RunThemeRoleEditor(role AS STRING)
    WHILE TRUE
        theme = ActiveTheme()
        roleEntry = Object.Get(theme, role)
        ignored = Display.Style(role)
        ignored = Display.Line("-- editing role: " + role + " --")
        ignored = Display.Reset()
        FOR tierName IN ThemeTierOrder()
            PRINT "  " + tierName + ": " + Object.Get(roleEntry, tierName)
        NEXT
        PRINT ""
        PRINT "colors: " + String.Join(NamedColorNames(), ", ") + ", default"
        PRINT "Pick a color by name, \"custom\" for raw per-tier codes, or \"b\" to go back."
        ignored = Console.Write(role + " color> ")
        line = Console.ReadLine()
        IF !line.Ok THEN RETURN
        choice = String.Trim(line.Text)
        lowered = Lower(choice)
        IF lowered == "" THEN CONTINUE WHILE
        IF lowered == "b" ORELSE lowered == "back" THEN RETURN

        IF lowered == "custom" THEN
            ignored = Console.Write("  TrueColor code: ")
            trueLine = Console.ReadLine()
            IF !trueLine.Ok THEN RETURN
            ignored = Console.Write("  Color256 code:  ")
            c256Line = Console.ReadLine()
            IF !c256Line.Ok THEN RETURN
            ignored = Console.Write("  Color16 code:   ")
            c16Line = Console.ReadLine()
            IF !c16Line.Ok THEN RETURN
            newEntry = {"TrueColor": String.Trim(trueLine.Text), "Color256": String.Trim(c256Line.Text),
                "Color16": String.Trim(c16Line.Text)}
            theme = Object.Set(theme, role, newEntry)
            ignored = SetActiveTheme(ActiveThemeName(), theme)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF lowered == "default" THEN
            baseTheme = DefaultTheme()
            theme = Object.Set(theme, role, Object.Get(baseTheme, role))
            ignored = SetActiveTheme(ActiveThemeName(), theme)
            ignored = Display.Style(role)
            ignored = Display.Line("  reset to default -- preview: " + role)
            ignored = Display.Reset()
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        bold = FALSE
        ignored = Console.Write("  bold? (y/N): ")
        boldLine = Console.ReadLine()
        IF !boldLine.Ok THEN RETURN
        IF Lower(String.Trim(boldLine.Text)) == "y" THEN bold = TRUE

        applied = ApplyNamedColorToRole(theme, role, choice, bold)
        IF !applied.Ok THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: unknown color: " + choice)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF
        theme = applied.Theme
        ignored = SetActiveTheme(ActiveThemeName(), theme)
        ignored = Display.Style(role)
        ignored = Display.Line("  updated -- preview: " + role)
        ignored = Display.Reset()
    WEND
END FUNCTION

' The interactive `theme edit` top-level menu: preview, pick a role by number or name (see
' ResolveRoleSelection), or save/quit. Nothing here writes to disk except the explicit "s" step,
' which mirrors "theme save <name>" exactly (refusing to overwrite the built-in "default" name).
FUNCTION RunThemeEditor()
    WHILE TRUE
        ignored = PrintThemePreview()
        roles = ThemeRoleOrder()
        PRINT ""
        PRINT "Select a role to edit by number or name, \"s\" to save this theme, or \"q\" to stop editing."
        i = 0
        WHILE i < LEN(roles)
            PRINT "  " + STRING(i + 1) + ". " + roles[i]
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        ignored = Console.Write("theme> ")
        line = Console.ReadLine()
        IF !line.Ok THEN RETURN
        choice = String.Trim(line.Text)
        lowered = Lower(choice)
        IF lowered == "" THEN CONTINUE WHILE
        IF lowered == "q" ORELSE lowered == "quit" THEN RETURN

        IF lowered == "s" ORELSE lowered == "save" THEN
            ignored = Console.Write("save as (blank keeps current name \"" + ActiveThemeName() + "\"): ")
            nameLine = Console.ReadLine()
            IF !nameLine.Ok THEN RETURN
            name = String.Trim(nameLine.Text)
            IF name == "" THEN name = ActiveThemeName()
            IF name == "default" THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: cannot save over the built-in \"default\" theme; choose another name")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = SaveTheme(name, ActiveTheme())
            ignored = SetActiveTheme(name, ActiveTheme())
            ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: theme: saved " + name)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        role = ResolveRoleSelection(choice, roles)
        IF role == "" THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: not a role or command: " + choice)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF
        ignored = RunThemeRoleEditor(role)
    WEND
END FUNCTION

' `theme` [edit | list | set <role> <tier> <code> | save <name> | use <name>]. Bare form previews
' the active theme by printing every required role's own name IN that role's own current color --
' a real, live swatch, not a description of one.
FUNCTION RunThemeCommand(arguments AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN
        ignored = PrintThemePreview()
        RETURN
    END IF

    subcommand = Upper(arguments[0])

    IF subcommand == "EDIT" THEN
        ignored = RunThemeEditor()
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "LIST" THEN
        names = ["default"]
        listed = Directory.List(ThemesDir())
        FOR entry IN listed.Entries
            ignored = Array.Push(names, entry)
        NEXT
        activeName = ActiveThemeName()
        FOR themeName IN names
            marker = "  "
            IF themeName == activeName THEN marker = "* "
            PRINT marker + themeName
        NEXT
        FOR cap IN Shell.PluginCapabilities()
            IF cap.Capability == "theme" THEN
                marker = "  "
                IF cap.Name == activeName THEN marker = "* "
                PRINT marker + cap.Name + " (plugin: " + cap.Plugin + ")"
            END IF
        NEXT
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "SET" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 4 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: usage: theme set <role> <tier> <code>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        role = Lower(arguments[1])
        tier = arguments[2]
        code = arguments[3]
        IF !Array.Contains(ThemeRoleOrder(), role) THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: unknown role: " + role)
            RETURN
        END IF
        IF !Array.Contains(ThemeTierOrder(), tier) THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: unknown tier: " + tier + " (expected TrueColor, Color256, or Color16)")
            RETURN
        END IF
        theme = ActiveTheme()
        roleEntry = Object.Get(theme, role)
        roleEntry = Object.Set(roleEntry, tier, code)
        theme = Object.Set(theme, role, roleEntry)
        ignored = SetActiveTheme(ActiveThemeName(), theme)
        ignored = Display.Style(role)
        ignored = Display.Line("updated " + role + " (" + tier + "): " + role)
        ignored = Display.Reset()
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "SAVE" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: usage: theme save <name>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = arguments[1]
        ignored = SaveTheme(name, ActiveTheme())
        ignored = SetActiveTheme(name, ActiveTheme())
        ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: theme: saved " + name)
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "USE" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: usage: theme use <name>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = arguments[1]
        ' File.Exists/Shell.PluginHasCapability are both Boxed host-function results -- combined
        ' here via nested IF, never ANDALSO/ORELSE directly on the raw Boxed value, for the same
        ' disclosed reason established throughout this file (see is_tty's own startup detection).
        themeKnown = FALSE
        IF name == "default" THEN themeKnown = TRUE
        IF !themeKnown THEN
            IF File.Exists(ThemePath(name)) THEN themeKnown = TRUE
        END IF
        IF !themeKnown THEN
            IF Shell.PluginHasCapability("theme", name) THEN themeKnown = TRUE
        END IF
        IF !themeKnown THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: no such theme: " + name)
            RETURN
        END IF
        ignored = SetActiveTheme(name, LoadTheme(name))
        ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: theme: now using " + name)
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: theme: unknown subcommand: " + arguments[0] + " (see \"help themes\")")
END FUNCTION

' Pure (no globals, no I/O) segment-list mutators shared by RunPromptEditor and RunEditorSelfTest.
' Array.Push/RemoveAt/Insert all mutate their first argument'S UNDERLYING ARRAY IN PLACE
' (Value::Array is a shared_ptr, unlike Value::Object -- see the comment on ParseThemeText's own
' Object.Set usage above for the opposite case), so "Segments" in the result is the SAME array as
' the "segments" argument, already updated -- returned anyway so every call site can use the
' result uniformly whether or not the input happened to be freshly constructed.
FUNCTION AddPromptSegment(segments AS ARRAY, name AS STRING)
    IF !Array.Contains(KnownPromptSegments(), name) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments,
            "Error": "unknown segment: " + name + " (known: " + String.Join(KnownPromptSegments(), ", ") + ")"}
    END IF
    IF Array.Contains(segments, name) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments, "Error": "segment already active: " + name}
    END IF
    ignored = Array.Push(segments, name)
    RETURN {"Ok": TRUE, "Segments": segments, "Error": ""}
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RemovePromptSegmentAt(segments AS ARRAY, index AS NUMBER)
    IF index < 0 ORELSE index >= LEN(segments) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments, "Error": "no such segment number: " + STRING(index + 1)}
    END IF
    IF LEN(segments) <= 1 THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments, "Error": "at least one segment must remain"}
    END IF
    ignored = Array.RemoveAt(segments, index)
    RETURN {"Ok": TRUE, "Segments": segments, "Error": ""}
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION MovePromptSegment(segments AS ARRAY, index AS NUMBER, direction AS STRING)
    IF index < 0 ORELSE index >= LEN(segments) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments, "Error": "no such segment number: " + STRING(index + 1)}
    END IF
    target = index - 1
    IF direction == "down" THEN target = index + 1
    IF target < 0 ORELSE target >= LEN(segments) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Segments": segments, "Error": "can't move that far"}
    END IF
    moving = Array.RemoveAt(segments, index)
    ignored = Array.Insert(segments, target, moving)
    RETURN {"Ok": TRUE, "Segments": segments, "Error": ""}
END FUNCTION

' The interactive `prompt edit` loop: add/remove/reorder one segment at a time with a live preview
' after every change, until "q" (or EOF) or an explicit "s" to persist it as the default for future
' sessions (the same thing "profile save" does with the whole session's settings, offered here too
' since a persisted prompt is the entire point of designing one).
FUNCTION RunPromptEditor(cwd AS STRING)
    WHILE TRUE
        segments = ActivePromptSegments()
        tags = ActivePromptTags()
        PRINT ""
        PRINT "Active prompt segments (in order):"
        i = 0
        WHILE i < LEN(segments)
            segLabel = "  " + STRING(i + 1) + ". " + segments[i]
            tag = Object.Get(tags, segments[i], "")
            IF tag <> "" ANDALSO tag <> "default" THEN segLabel = segLabel + " (tag: " + tag + ")"
            PRINT segLabel
            i = i + 1
        WEND
        PRINT "Preview: " + Prompt(cwd, 0, [])
        PRINT ""
        PRINT "Known segments: " + String.Join(KnownPromptSegments(), ", ")
        PRINT "Colors:         " + String.Join(ThemeRoleOrder(), ", ")
        PRINT "Commands: add <segment>, remove <number>, move <number> up|down,"
        PRINT "          tag <segment> <color|default>, s(ave), q(uit)"
        ignored = Console.Write("prompt> ")
        line = Console.ReadLine()
        IF !line.Ok THEN RETURN
        raw = String.Trim(line.Text)
        IF raw == "" THEN CONTINUE WHILE
        lowered = Lower(raw)
        IF lowered == "q" ORELSE lowered == "quit" THEN RETURN

        IF lowered == "s" ORELSE lowered == "save" THEN
            ignored = SaveConfig(CurrentConfigFromSession())
            ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: prompt: saved (this is now the default for new sessions)")
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        parts = String.Split(raw, " ")
        verb = Lower(parts[0])

        IF verb == "add" THEN
            IF LEN(parts) < 2 THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: add <segment>")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            result = AddPromptSegment(segments, Lower(parts[1]))
            IF !result.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: " + result.Error)
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(result.Segments)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF verb == "remove" THEN
            IF LEN(parts) < 2 ORELSE !IsAllDigits(parts[1]) THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: remove <number>")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            result = RemovePromptSegmentAt(segments, NUMBER(parts[1]) - 1)
            IF !result.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: " + result.Error)
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(result.Segments)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF verb == "move" THEN
            IF LEN(parts) < 3 ORELSE !IsAllDigits(parts[1]) THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: move <number> up|down")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            direction = Lower(parts[2])
            IF direction <> "up" ANDALSO direction <> "down" THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: move <number> up|down")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            result = MovePromptSegment(segments, NUMBER(parts[1]) - 1, direction)
            IF !result.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: " + result.Error)
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(result.Segments)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        IF verb == "tag" THEN
            IF LEN(parts) < 3 THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: tag <segment> <color|default>")
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            result = TagPromptSegment(tags, Lower(parts[1]), Lower(parts[2]))
            IF !result.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: " + result.Error)
                CONTINUE WHILE
            END IF
            ignored = SetActivePromptTags(result.Tags)
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF

        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: unknown command: " + raw)
    WEND
END FUNCTION

' `prompt` [edit | set <seg1,seg2,...>]. Bare form shows the active segment list and a live
' preview of the resulting prompt text.
FUNCTION RunPromptCommand(cwd AS STRING, arguments AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN
        PRINT "active segments: " + String.Join(ActivePromptSegments(), ", ")
        PRINT "known segments:  " + String.Join(KnownPromptSegments(), ", ")
        tagsText = SerializePromptTags(ActivePromptTags())
        IF tagsText == "" THEN tagsText = "(none)"
        PRINT "color tags:      " + tagsText
        PRINT "preview:         " + Prompt(cwd, 0, [])
        RETURN
    END IF

    subcommand = Upper(arguments[0])

    IF subcommand == "EDIT" THEN
        ignored = RunPromptEditor(cwd)
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "SET" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: prompt set <seg1,seg2,...>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        requested = []
        FOR seg IN String.Split(arguments[1], ",")
            ignored = Array.Push(requested, String.Trim(seg))
        NEXT
        FOR seg IN requested
            IF !Array.Contains(KnownPromptSegments(), seg) THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: unknown segment: " + seg + " (known: " + String.Join(KnownPromptSegments(), ", ") + ")")
                RETURN
            END IF
        NEXT
        ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(requested)
        PRINT "preview: " + Prompt(cwd, 0, [])
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "TAG" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 3 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: usage: prompt tag <segment> <role> (role \"default\" clears a tag)")
            RETURN
        END IF
        result = TagPromptSegment(ActivePromptTags(), Lower(arguments[1]), Lower(arguments[2]))
        IF !result.Ok THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: " + result.Error)
            RETURN
        END IF
        ignored = SetActivePromptTags(result.Tags)
        PRINT "preview: " + Prompt(cwd, 0, [])
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: prompt: unknown subcommand: " + arguments[0] + " (see \"help prompt-customization\")")
END FUNCTION

' `profile` [show | save [name] | load <name> | list]. The MAIN config (ConfigPath) is always
' what gets applied at the next startup; a NAMED profile is a separate saved snapshot under
' ProfilesDir a later "profile load <name>" can bring back -- "profile save" with no name just
' updates the main config, "profile save <name>" does that AND takes a named snapshot.
'
' NoColor here is CurrentColorTier() == "None" -- the session's CURRENT EFFECTIVE color state,
' which also collapses to None from a one-off --no-color flag or a genuinely no-color-capable
' terminal, not only from a previously-saved preference. This is disclosed, deliberate behavior,
' not a bug: "profile save" always means "make what's active right now the default", the same way
' saving current settings works in most tools -- running once with --no-color and then saving
' really will make future sessions start in no-color too. Documented in "help profiles" rather
' than adding a separate always-vs-just-now distinction nothing has asked for yet.
FUNCTION CurrentConfigFromSession()
    RETURN {"Theme": ActiveThemeName(), "PromptSegments": ActivePromptSegments(),
        "PromptTags": ActivePromptTags(), "NoColor": CurrentColorTier() == "None"}
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RunProfileCommand(arguments AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN
        config = CurrentConfigFromSession()
        PRINT "theme:           " + config.Theme
        PRINT "prompt segments: " + String.Join(config.PromptSegments, ", ")
        tagsText = SerializePromptTags(config.PromptTags)
        IF tagsText == "" THEN tagsText = "(none)"
        PRINT "prompt tags:     " + tagsText
        PRINT "no-color:        " + STRING(config.NoColor)
        RETURN
    END IF

    subcommand = Upper(arguments[0])

    IF subcommand == "SAVE" THEN
        config = CurrentConfigFromSession()
        ignored = SaveConfig(config)
        IF LEN(arguments) >= 2 THEN
            name = arguments[1]
            ignored = EnsureProfileDirectories()
            ignored = File.WriteText(ProfilePath(name), SerializeConfig(config))
            ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: profile: saved as \"" + name + "\" and made active")
        ELSE
            ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: profile: saved")
        END IF
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "LOAD" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: profile: usage: profile load <name>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = arguments[1]
        path = ProfilePath(name)
        IF !File.Exists(path) THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: profile: no such profile: " + name)
            RETURN
        END IF
        config = ParseConfigText(File.ReadText(path))
        ignored = SaveConfig(config)
        ignored = SetActiveTheme(config.Theme, LoadTheme(config.Theme))
        ignored = SetActivePromptSegments(config.PromptSegments)
        ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: profile: loaded \"" + name + "\" (color policy applies on next start)")
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "LIST" THEN
        listed = Directory.List(ProfilesDir())
        IF LEN(listed.Entries) == 0 THEN
            PRINT "(no saved profiles)"
        END IF
        FOR entry IN listed.Entries
            PRINT "  " + entry
        NEXT
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: profile: unknown subcommand: " + arguments[0] + " (see \"help profiles\")")
END FUNCTION

' Structural + real-file regression coverage -- runs against a real, temporary ARCOSH_HOME
' (never the real user's own ~/.arcosh) so it can freely create/overwrite files.
FUNCTION RunProfileSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    testHome = Path.Cwd() + "/wp_profile_selftest_home"
    ignored = Process.Execute("rm", ["-rf", testHome])
    ignored = Directory.Create(testHome)

    ' ParseDeclarativeLines / config round-trip.
    config = {"Theme": "mytheme", "PromptSegments": ["path", "hostname"],
        "PromptTags": {"hostname": "error"}, "NoColor": TRUE}
    serialized = SerializeConfig(config)
    reparsed = ParseConfigText(serialized)
    configMatches = reparsed.Theme == "mytheme"
    IF configMatches THEN configMatches = LEN(reparsed.PromptSegments) == 2
    IF configMatches THEN configMatches = reparsed.PromptSegments[0] == "path"
    IF configMatches THEN configMatches = Object.Get(reparsed.PromptTags, "hostname") == "error"
    IF configMatches THEN configMatches = reparsed.NoColor
    IF configMatches THEN
        PRINT "ok   config (including prompt color tags) round-trips through Serialize/Parse correctly"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL config did not round-trip correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Theme round-trip: a partial custom theme still has every role/tier from DefaultTheme.
    customTheme = DefaultTheme()
    errorRole = Object.Get(customTheme, "error")
    errorRole = Object.Set(errorRole, "TrueColor", "1;38;2;255;0;0")
    customTheme = Object.Set(customTheme, "error", errorRole)
    themeText = SerializeTheme(customTheme)
    reparsedTheme = ParseThemeText(themeText)
    reparsedError = Object.Get(reparsedTheme, "error")
    reparsedNormal = Object.Get(reparsedTheme, "normal")
    themeMatches = Object.Get(reparsedError, "TrueColor") == "1;38;2;255;0;0"
    IF themeMatches THEN themeMatches = Object.Get(reparsedNormal, "TrueColor") == Object.Get(Object.Get(DefaultTheme(), "normal"), "TrueColor")
    IF themeMatches THEN
        PRINT "ok   theme round-trips, preserving the edit and every other role's default"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL theme did not round-trip correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Real file save/load, using a real temporary profile home (ProfileHome() itself just reads
    ' $ARCOSH_HOME/$HOME, which ArcoBASIC has no way to override for THIS process's own
    ' environment -- verified manually instead, launching a real arcosh with ARCOSH_HOME set).
    themePath = testHome + "/themes/selftest-theme"
    ignored = Directory.Create(testHome + "/themes")
    ignored = File.WriteText(themePath, themeText)
    loadedFromDisk = ParseThemeText(File.ReadText(themePath))
    IF Object.Get(Object.Get(loadedFromDisk, "error"), "TrueColor") == "1;38;2;255;0;0" THEN
        PRINT "ok   a theme written to disk reads back correctly"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a theme written to disk did not read back correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' RenderPromptSegment: classic reproduces the exact pre-customization text; unknown segments
    ' and an empty job table both produce "" rather than garbage.
    plainContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "None", "", {}, "testuser")
    classicRendered = RenderPromptSegment("classic", plainContext)
    IF classicRendered == "arcosh::tmp" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the classic segment renders exactly like the pre-customization prompt"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL classic segment rendered: [" + classicRendered + "] (expected \"arcosh::tmp\")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF RenderPromptSegment("jobs", plainContext) == "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the jobs segment is empty when there are no jobs"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL jobs segment was not empty with zero jobs"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF RenderPromptSegment("not-a-real-segment", plainContext) == "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   an unrecognized segment renders as nothing instead of crashing"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL an unrecognized segment did not render as nothing"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' date/time segments -- Time.Now()'s fixed "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" format sliced into its two
    ' halves; not asserting an exact VALUE (the real wall clock, not under this test's control),
    ' just the expected LENGTH and separator position, which is what a format regression would break.
    dateRendered = RenderPromptSegment("date", plainContext)
    IF LEN(dateRendered) == 10 ANDALSO String.Slice(dateRendered, 4, 1) == "-" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the date segment renders a 10-character YYYY-MM-DD date"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL date segment rendered: [" + dateRendered + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    timeRendered = RenderPromptSegment("time", plainContext)
    IF LEN(timeRendered) == 8 ANDALSO String.Slice(timeRendered, 2, 1) == ":" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the time segment renders an 8-character HH:MM:SS time"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL time segment rendered: [" + timeRendered + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Broken-down date/time fields: each checked for the right WIDTH and all-digits, not an exact
    ' value (the real wall clock, not under this test's control). year/month/day/hour/minute/
    ' second must also each be a SUBSTRING of the combined "date"/"time" segments rendered in the
    ' same instant, proving they're sliced from the exact same fields, not independently wrong in
    ' a way that happens to still be the right length.
    combinedDate = String.Slice(RenderPromptSegment("date", plainContext), 0, 10)
    combinedTime = String.Slice(RenderPromptSegment("time", plainContext), 0, 8)
    fieldsOk = TRUE
    FOR fieldSpec IN [{"Name": "year", "Width": 4}, {"Name": "month", "Width": 2}, {"Name": "day", "Width": 2},
        {"Name": "hour", "Width": 2}, {"Name": "minute", "Width": 2}, {"Name": "second", "Width": 2}]
        rendered = RenderPromptSegment(fieldSpec.Name, plainContext)
        IF LEN(rendered) <> fieldSpec.Width THEN fieldsOk = FALSE
        IF !IsAllDigits(rendered) THEN fieldsOk = FALSE
    NEXT
    IF fieldsOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   year/month/day/hour/minute/second each render as the right width, all digits"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a broken-down date/time field had the wrong width or non-digit content"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    year2Rendered = RenderPromptSegment("year2", plainContext)
    IF LEN(year2Rendered) == 2 ANDALSO IsAllDigits(year2Rendered) ANDALSO String.Slice(combinedDate, 2, 2) == year2Rendered THEN
        PRINT "ok   year2 renders the last 2 digits of the 4-digit year"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL year2 rendered: [" + year2Rendered + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF String.Slice(combinedTime, 0, 2) == RenderPromptSegment("hour", plainContext) THEN
        PRINT "ok   the hour field agrees with the combined time segment"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL the hour field did not agree with the combined time segment"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF PadNumber(7, 3) == "007" ANDALSO PadNumber(42, 3) == "042" ANDALSO PadNumber(999, 3) == "999" THEN
        PRINT "ok   PadNumber zero-pads to the requested width without truncating a wider number"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL PadNumber did not pad correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    millisRendered = RenderPromptSegment("millisecond", plainContext)
    IF LEN(millisRendered) == 3 ANDALSO IsAllDigits(millisRendered) THEN
        PRINT "ok   the millisecond segment renders a zero-padded 3-digit value"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL millisecond segment rendered: [" + millisRendered + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' Color tags: an explicit tag overrides a segment's own default role, "default" clears it back,
    ' and exitcode's own dynamic success/error behavior still applies when untagged.
    taggedTheme = ParseThemeText("")
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_active_theme", taggedTheme)
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_color_tier", "TrueColor")
    tagResult = TagPromptSegment({}, "hostname", "error")
    IF tagResult.Ok THEN
        taggedContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "TrueColor", "", tagResult.Tags, "testuser")
        untaggedContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "TrueColor", "", {}, "testuser")
        taggedHostname = RenderPromptSegment("hostname", taggedContext)
        untaggedHostname = RenderPromptSegment("hostname", untaggedContext)
        IF taggedHostname == StyleCode("error", "TrueColor") + Host.Hostname() ANDALSO taggedHostname <> untaggedHostname THEN
            PRINT "ok   TagPromptSegment overrides a segment's rendered color role"
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL a tagged segment did not render in the tagged role"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL TagPromptSegment rejected a valid segment/role pair"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !TagPromptSegment({}, "not-a-real-segment", "error").Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   TagPromptSegment rejects an unknown segment"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL TagPromptSegment accepted an unknown segment"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !TagPromptSegment({}, "hostname", "not-a-real-role").Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   TagPromptSegment rejects an unknown role"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL TagPromptSegment accepted an unknown role"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    untaggedExitContext0 = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "None", "", {}, "testuser")
    untaggedExitContext1 = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 1, [], "None", "", {}, "testuser")
    exitOk = ResolveSegmentRole(untaggedExitContext0, "exitcode") == "success"
    IF exitOk THEN exitOk = ResolveSegmentRole(untaggedExitContext1, "exitcode") == "error"
    IF exitOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   an untagged exitcode segment keeps its own dynamic success/error role"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL untagged exitcode did not resolve to success/error correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    pinnedExit = TagPromptSegment({}, "exitcode", "muted")
    pinnedExitContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 1, [], "None", "", pinnedExit.Tags, "testuser")
    IF pinnedExit.Ok ANDALSO ResolveSegmentRole(pinnedExitContext, "exitcode") == "muted" THEN
        PRINT "ok   a tagged exitcode segment uses the fixed tagged role instead of dynamic coloring"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a tagged exitcode segment did not use its fixed tagged role"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' "user" segment: renders whatever username it's given (never re-deriving it -- Host.CurrentUser
    ' itself isn't under this test's control), defaults to "error" for root and "prompt" otherwise,
    ' and an explicit tag still overrides both.
    sysadminContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "TrueColor", "", {}, "sysadmin")
    rootContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "TrueColor", "", {}, "root")
    userRendered = RenderPromptSegment("user", sysadminContext)
    IF userRendered == StyleCode("prompt", "TrueColor") + "sysadmin" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the user segment renders the given username in the prompt role by default"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL user segment rendered: [" + userRendered + "] (expected prompt-styled \"sysadmin\")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    rootRendered = RenderPromptSegment("user", rootContext)
    IF rootRendered == StyleCode("error", "TrueColor") + "root" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the user segment defaults to the error role when running as root"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL root user segment rendered: [" + rootRendered + "] (expected error-styled \"root\")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    userTag = TagPromptSegment({}, "user", "muted")
    taggedRootContext = MakePromptContext("/tmp", 0, [], "TrueColor", "", userTag.Tags, "root")
    IF userTag.Ok ANDALSO ResolveSegmentRole(taggedRootContext, "user") == "muted" THEN
        PRINT "ok   an explicit user tag overrides even the root default"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL tagging the user segment did not override the root default"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ignored = Process.Execute("rm", ["-rf", testHome])
    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' Structural coverage for the theme/prompt EDITORS ("theme edit" / "prompt edit"): every function
' the interactive loops delegate to for actual color/segment mutation is pure (no globals, no I/O)
' by design specifically so it can be tested directly here, without needing to fake stdin for the
' loops themselves -- the loops' own Console.ReadLine-driven menu behavior is verified manually
' against the compiled binary under a real pty instead (see arcosh/README.md), the same split
' WP-012's own RunJobControlSelfTest comment explains for a real Ctrl-Z.
FUNCTION RunEditorSelfTest()
    failures = 0

    IF IsAllDigits("42") THEN
        PRINT "ok   IsAllDigits accepts a plain number"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL IsAllDigits rejected a plain number"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !IsAllDigits("") ANDALSO !IsAllDigits("4a") ANDALSO !IsAllDigits("q") THEN
        PRINT "ok   IsAllDigits rejects empty/mixed/non-numeric text"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL IsAllDigits accepted non-numeric text"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF Object.Has(FindNamedColor("not-a-real-color"), "IsNothing") THEN
        PRINT "ok   FindNamedColor reports an unrecognized name as not found"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL FindNamedColor did not reject an unrecognized name"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    red = FindNamedColor("RED")
    IF !Object.Has(red, "IsNothing") ANDALSO red.Name == "red" THEN
        PRINT "ok   FindNamedColor is case-insensitive and finds a real color"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL FindNamedColor did not find \"RED\""
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    applied = ApplyNamedColorToRole(DefaultTheme(), "path", "purple", FALSE)
    appliedOk = applied.Ok
    IF appliedOk THEN appliedOk = Object.Get(applied.Theme, "path").TrueColor == FindNamedColor("purple").TrueColor
    IF appliedOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   ApplyNamedColorToRole sets the role to the named color's codes"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ApplyNamedColorToRole did not apply purple to path"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    boldApplied = ApplyNamedColorToRole(DefaultTheme(), "path", "purple", TRUE)
    IF boldApplied.Ok ANDALSO Object.Get(boldApplied.Theme, "path").Color16 == "1;35" THEN
        PRINT "ok   ApplyNamedColorToRole prefixes 1; for bold"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ApplyNamedColorToRole did not apply the bold prefix"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    unknownApplied = ApplyNamedColorToRole(DefaultTheme(), "path", "not-a-real-color", FALSE)
    IF !unknownApplied.Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   ApplyNamedColorToRole reports failure for an unrecognized color, theme untouched"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ApplyNamedColorToRole accepted an unrecognized color"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    roles = ThemeRoleOrder()
    IF ResolveRoleSelection("1", roles) == roles[0] THEN
        PRINT "ok   ResolveRoleSelection accepts a 1-based menu number"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ResolveRoleSelection did not resolve menu number 1"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF ResolveRoleSelection("ERROR", roles) == "error" THEN
        PRINT "ok   ResolveRoleSelection accepts a role name, case-insensitively"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ResolveRoleSelection did not resolve \"ERROR\""
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF ResolveRoleSelection("99", roles) == "" ANDALSO ResolveRoleSelection("nope", roles) == "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   ResolveRoleSelection returns \"\" for an out-of-range number or unknown name"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ResolveRoleSelection accepted an invalid selection"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    added = AddPromptSegment(["classic"], "hostname")
    IF added.Ok ANDALSO LEN(added.Segments) == 2 ANDALSO added.Segments[1] == "hostname" THEN
        PRINT "ok   AddPromptSegment appends a known, not-yet-active segment"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL AddPromptSegment did not append hostname"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !AddPromptSegment(["classic"], "not-a-real-segment").Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   AddPromptSegment rejects an unknown segment name"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL AddPromptSegment accepted an unknown segment name"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !AddPromptSegment(["classic", "path"], "path").Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   AddPromptSegment rejects a segment that's already active"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL AddPromptSegment accepted a duplicate segment"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    removed = RemovePromptSegmentAt(["path", "hostname", "jobs"], 1)
    IF removed.Ok ANDALSO LEN(removed.Segments) == 2 ANDALSO removed.Segments[0] == "path" ANDALSO removed.Segments[1] == "jobs" THEN
        PRINT "ok   RemovePromptSegmentAt removes the segment at the given index"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL RemovePromptSegmentAt did not remove index 1 correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !RemovePromptSegmentAt(["classic"], 0).Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   RemovePromptSegmentAt refuses to remove the last remaining segment"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL RemovePromptSegmentAt allowed removing the last segment"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !RemovePromptSegmentAt(["path"], 5).Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   RemovePromptSegmentAt refuses an out-of-range index"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL RemovePromptSegmentAt accepted an out-of-range index"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    movedUp = MovePromptSegment(["path", "hostname", "jobs"], 2, "up")
    IF movedUp.Ok ANDALSO movedUp.Segments[0] == "path" ANDALSO movedUp.Segments[1] == "jobs" ANDALSO movedUp.Segments[2] == "hostname" THEN
        PRINT "ok   MovePromptSegment moves a segment up (toward index 0)"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL MovePromptSegment did not move the segment up correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    movedDown = MovePromptSegment(["path", "hostname", "jobs"], 0, "down")
    IF movedDown.Ok ANDALSO movedDown.Segments[0] == "hostname" ANDALSO movedDown.Segments[1] == "path" THEN
        PRINT "ok   MovePromptSegment moves a segment down"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL MovePromptSegment did not move the segment down correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !MovePromptSegment(["path", "hostname"], 0, "up").Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   MovePromptSegment refuses to move past the start of the list"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL MovePromptSegment allowed moving past the start of the list"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Plugins (RFC-0052 WP-009, section 19). Real, dynamically-loaded `.abas` plugin files, discovered
' under ~/.arcosh/plugins (or $ARCOSH_HOME/plugins), that register genuine, repeatedly-invokable
' capabilities across four typed classes (`command`, `prompt.segment`, `theme`, `completion`) --
' not just static data. This needed real research before implementation (AP-0052-012's own
' requirement, matching WP-012's precedent): see
' .agents/reports/ARCO_SH_RFC0052_WP009_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_RESEARCH.md for the load-bearing finding --
' a plugin's callback stays genuinely invokable, later, from this natively-compiled program, ONLY
' because plugin source is run through one dedicated, process-lifetime interpreter
' (Shell.LoadPlugin/PluginRegister/PluginInvoke/PluginCapabilities/PluginHasCapability/
' PluginCapabilityValue, src/runtime/runtime.cpp), never Runtime.RunString's own disposable one.
'
' Plugins register through plain ArcoBASIC wrapper functions matching RFC-0052 section 19.1's own
' conceptual API literally (`Shell.RegisterCommand`, `Shell.RegisterPromptSegment`,
' `Shell.RegisterTheme`, `Shell.RegisterCompleter`) -- these are defined by PluginPrelude() below
' and prepended to every plugin's own source before it runs, so a plugin author never needs to know
' about the underlying Shell.PluginRegister primitive at all.
'
' `completion` is registered and enumerable today (satisfying section 19's own capability-class
' list) but nothing in ArcoSH yet CONSUMES a registered completer -- there is no tab-completion
' system at all yet (that is WP-010's own, separate work package). Disclosed here and in "help
' plugins" rather than silently pretending it does something.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' Prepended to every plugin's own source (see LoadOnePlugin) so plugin authors write the RFC's own
' literal `Shell.RegisterX(name, ...)` calls -- each just forwards to the real Shell.PluginRegister
' primitive with the right capability-class string baked in.
FUNCTION PluginPrelude()
    RETURN String.Join([
        "FUNCTION Shell.RegisterCommand(name AS STRING, callback AS CALLABLE)",
        "    pluginPreludeIgnored = Shell.PluginRegister(" + Chr(34) + "command" + Chr(34) + ", name, callback)",
        "END FUNCTION",
        "FUNCTION Shell.RegisterPromptSegment(name AS STRING, callback AS CALLABLE)",
        "    pluginPreludeIgnored = Shell.PluginRegister(" + Chr(34) + "prompt.segment" + Chr(34) + ", name, callback)",
        "END FUNCTION",
        "FUNCTION Shell.RegisterTheme(name AS STRING, theme AS OBJECT)",
        "    pluginPreludeIgnored = Shell.PluginRegister(" + Chr(34) + "theme" + Chr(34) + ", name, theme)",
        "END FUNCTION",
        "FUNCTION Shell.RegisterCompleter(name AS STRING, callback AS CALLABLE)",
        "    pluginPreludeIgnored = Shell.PluginRegister(" + Chr(34) + "completion" + Chr(34) + ", name, callback)",
        "END FUNCTION"
    ], "\n")
END FUNCTION

' Every installed plugin's own base name (filename without ".abas"), sorted -- AP-0052-012's own
' "deterministic load order" requirement -- regardless of enabled/disabled state; RunPluginsCommand
' and LoadEnabledPlugins both filter this same list down to what they each need.
FUNCTION DiscoverPluginNames()
    names = []
    listed = Directory.List(PluginsDir())
    FOR entry IN listed.Entries
        IF String.EndsWith(entry, ".abas") THEN
            ignored = Array.Push(names, String.Slice(entry, 0, LEN(entry) - 5))
        END IF
    NEXT
    RETURN Array.Sort(names)
END FUNCTION

' enabled.txt: one plugin name per line, blank lines and "#" comments skipped -- plain text, same
' spirit as config/theme files (RFC-0052 section 22), since which plugins are active is themselves
' a piece of configuration, not executable code.
FUNCTION ParsePluginNameList(text AS STRING)
    names = []
    FOR rawLine IN String.Split(text, "\n")
        line = String.Trim(rawLine)
        IF line == "" THEN CONTINUE FOR
        IF String.Slice(line, 0, 1) == "#" THEN CONTINUE FOR
        IF !Array.Contains(names, line) THEN ignored = Array.Push(names, line)
    NEXT
    RETURN names
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SerializePluginNameList(names AS ARRAY)
    RETURN String.Join(names, "\n")
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION LoadEnabledPluginNames()
    path = PluginsEnabledPath()
    IF !File.Exists(path) THEN RETURN []
    RETURN ParsePluginNameList(File.ReadText(path))
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION SaveEnabledPluginNames(names AS ARRAY)
    ignored = EnsurePluginsDirectory()
    ignored = File.WriteText(PluginsEnabledPath(), SerializePluginNameList(names))
END FUNCTION

' Loads exactly one named, already-installed plugin: prepends PluginPrelude() so its own
' Shell.RegisterX calls resolve, then runs the whole thing through Shell.LoadPlugin (the one
' persistent plugin interpreter -- see this section's own header comment). Never throws -- a
' missing file is reported the same {Name, Ok, Error} way a real load failure is, so every caller
' can treat "not installed" and "installed but broken" uniformly.
FUNCTION LoadOnePlugin(name AS STRING)
    path = PluginFilePath(name)
    IF !File.Exists(path) THEN
        RETURN {"Name": name, "Ok": FALSE, "Error": "plugin file not found: " + path}
    END IF
    fullSource = PluginPrelude() + "\n" + File.ReadText(path)
    result = Shell.LoadPlugin(name, fullSource)
    RETURN {"Name": name, "Ok": result.Ok, "Error": result.Error}
END FUNCTION

' Called once at startup (unless --no-plugins -- RFC-0052 section 19.3's own required "recovery
' launch with optional plugins disabled"): loads every ENABLED, discovered plugin in deterministic
' (sorted) order, printing a warning for each one that fails to load but continuing with the rest
' -- section 19.3's own "a broken optional plugin SHOULD NOT make the shell unrecoverable" -- and
' records the whole outcome for "plugins" to display later without reloading anything.
FUNCTION LoadEnabledPlugins()
    report = []
    enabledNames = LoadEnabledPluginNames()
    FOR name IN DiscoverPluginNames()
        IF Array.Contains(enabledNames, name) THEN
            outcome = LoadOnePlugin(name)
            ignored = Array.Push(report, outcome)
            IF !outcome.Ok THEN ignored = DisplayWarning("arcosh: plugin " + name + " failed to load: " + outcome.Error)
        END IF
    NEXT
    ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_plugin_load_report", report)
    RETURN report
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION PluginLoadReport()
    report = Runtime.GetGlobal("arcosh_plugin_load_report")
    IF TYPEOF(report) <> "Array" THEN RETURN []
    RETURN report
END FUNCTION

' Pure (no globals/I/O -- see RunPluginSelfTest): {Capability, Name, Plugin} triples from
' Shell.PluginCapabilities(), grouped by which plugin registered each one, as
' {pluginName: ["capability.name", ...]} -- matches RFC-0052 section 19.2's own worked example
' ("GitTools" / "command.gitstatus", "completion.git", "prompt.segment.git" / "Neon" /
' "theme.neon") almost exactly.
FUNCTION GroupCapabilitiesByPlugin(capabilities AS ARRAY)
    grouped = {}
    FOR cap IN capabilities
        pluginName = cap.Plugin
        IF pluginName == "" THEN pluginName = "(unknown)"
        existing = Object.Get(grouped, pluginName, [])
        ignored = Array.Push(existing, cap.Capability + "." + cap.Name)
        grouped = Object.Set(grouped, pluginName, existing)
    NEXT
    RETURN grouped
END FUNCTION

' `plugins` [install <path> [name] | enable <name> | disable <name>]. Bare form lists every
' installed plugin with its enabled/disabled status and any load failure, then every currently
' registered capability grouped by plugin (section 19.2's own inspectability requirement).
FUNCTION RunPluginsCommand(arguments AS ARRAY)
    IF LEN(arguments) == 0 THEN
        discovered = DiscoverPluginNames()
        enabledNames = LoadEnabledPluginNames()
        IF LEN(discovered) == 0 THEN
            PRINT "(no plugins installed -- see \"help plugins\")"
        END IF
        FOR name IN discovered
            status = "disabled"
            IF Array.Contains(enabledNames, name) THEN status = "enabled"
            PRINT name + " (" + status + ")"
        NEXT
        FOR outcome IN PluginLoadReport()
            IF !outcome.Ok THEN PRINT "  " + outcome.Name + ": FAILED to load: " + outcome.Error
        NEXT
        grouped = GroupCapabilitiesByPlugin(Shell.PluginCapabilities())
        FOR pluginName IN Object.Keys(grouped)
            PRINT ""
            PRINT pluginName
            FOR capLabel IN Object.Get(grouped, pluginName, [])
                PRINT "  " + capLabel
            NEXT
        NEXT
        RETURN
    END IF

    subcommand = Upper(arguments[0])

    IF subcommand == "INSTALL" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: usage: plugins install <path> [name]")
            RETURN
        END IF
        sourcePath = arguments[1]
        IF !File.Exists(sourcePath) THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: no such file: " + sourcePath)
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = ""
        IF LEN(arguments) >= 3 THEN
            name = arguments[2]
        ELSE
            base = Path.BaseName(sourcePath)
            ext = Path.Extension(sourcePath)
            name = base
            IF ext <> "" THEN name = String.Slice(base, 0, LEN(base) - LEN(ext))
        END IF
        ignored = EnsurePluginsDirectory()
        ignored = File.WriteText(PluginFilePath(name), File.ReadText(sourcePath))
        ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: plugins: installed \"" + name + "\" (run \"plugins enable " + name + "\" to activate it)")
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "ENABLE" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: usage: plugins enable <name>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = arguments[1]
        IF !File.Exists(PluginFilePath(name)) THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: no such installed plugin: " + name + " (see \"plugins install\")")
            RETURN
        END IF
        enabledNames = LoadEnabledPluginNames()
        IF !Array.Contains(enabledNames, name) THEN ignored = Array.Push(enabledNames, name)
        ignored = SaveEnabledPluginNames(enabledNames)
        outcome = LoadOnePlugin(name)
        IF outcome.Ok THEN
            ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: plugins: enabled and loaded \"" + name + "\"")
        ELSE
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: enabled \"" + name + "\" but it failed to load: " + outcome.Error)
        END IF
        RETURN
    END IF

    IF subcommand == "DISABLE" THEN
        IF LEN(arguments) < 2 THEN
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: usage: plugins disable <name>")
            RETURN
        END IF
        name = arguments[1]
        enabledNames = LoadEnabledPluginNames()
        updated = []
        FOR existing IN enabledNames
            IF existing <> name THEN ignored = Array.Push(updated, existing)
        NEXT
        ignored = SaveEnabledPluginNames(updated)
        ignored = DisplaySuccess("arcosh: plugins: disabled \"" + name + "\" (takes effect on next start -- capabilities it already registered this session stay active until you restart)")
        RETURN
    END IF

    ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: plugins: unknown subcommand: " + arguments[0] + " (see \"help plugins\")")
END FUNCTION

' A registered `command` capability, invoked as a shell built-in (see the main loop's own dispatch
' below) -- a proof-of-concept integration, not deep pipeline/redirection support: a plugin command
' never sees pipes/redirection/backgrounding, only its own parsed Arguments, the real, disclosed
' scope AP-0052-012's own "provide at least one proof plugin" bar asks for. A plugin command that
' throws is caught here and reported, never crashing the shell (section 19.3).
FUNCTION RunPluginCommand(parsed AS OBJECT)
    TRY
        output = Shell.PluginInvoke("command", parsed.Executable, {"Arguments": parsed.Arguments})
        IF output <> "" THEN PRINT output
    CATCH err
        ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: " + parsed.Executable + ": plugin command failed: " + err.Message)
    END TRY
END FUNCTION

' Structural + real-plugin-load regression coverage, run against a real, temporary ARCOSH_HOME
' (never the real user's own ~/.arcosh) so it can freely install/enable plugins -- includes a
' deliberately BROKEN plugin (AP-0052-016's own explicit "plugin failure" required test area).
FUNCTION RunPluginSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    ignored = Shell.PluginResetForTesting()
    testHome = Path.Cwd() + "/wp_plugin_selftest_home"
    ignored = Process.Execute("rm", ["-rf", testHome])
    ignored = Directory.Create(testHome)

    ' ParsePluginNameList / SerializePluginNameList round-trip, including comments/blanks/dupes.
    parsedNames = ParsePluginNameList(String.Join(["gittools", "", "# a comment", "neon", "gittools"], "\n"))
    namesMatch = LEN(parsedNames) == 2
    IF namesMatch THEN namesMatch = parsedNames[0] == "gittools"
    IF namesMatch THEN namesMatch = parsedNames[1] == "neon"
    IF namesMatch THEN
        PRINT "ok   ParsePluginNameList skips blanks/comments and de-duplicates"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL ParsePluginNameList did not parse the name list correctly"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' A real plugin, installed to a real temporary plugins directory, registering all four
    ' capability classes through the RFC's own literal Shell.RegisterX API.
    pluginsDir = testHome + "/plugins"
    ignored = Directory.Create(pluginsDir)
    goodPlugin = String.Join([
        "FUNCTION RenderGitBranch(context AS OBJECT)",
        "    RETURN " + Chr(34) + "git:main" + Chr(34),
        "END FUNCTION",
        "FUNCTION RunGitStatus(args AS OBJECT)",
        "    RETURN " + Chr(34) + "nothing to commit" + Chr(34),
        "END FUNCTION",
        "ignored1 = Shell.RegisterPromptSegment(" + Chr(34) + "git" + Chr(34) + ", ADDRESSOF RenderGitBranch)",
        "ignored2 = Shell.RegisterCommand(" + Chr(34) + "gitstatus" + Chr(34) + ", ADDRESSOF RunGitStatus)",
        "ignored3 = Shell.RegisterTheme(" + Chr(34) + "neon" + Chr(34) + ", {" + Chr(34) + "error" + Chr(34) + ": {" + Chr(34) + "TrueColor" + Chr(34) + ": " + Chr(34) + "38;2;255;0;255" + Chr(34) + "}})"
    ], "\n")
    ignored = File.WriteText(pluginsDir + "/gittools.abas", goodPlugin)

    discovered = DiscoverPluginNames_TestOverride(pluginsDir)
    IF LEN(discovered) == 1 ANDALSO discovered[0] == "gittools" THEN
        PRINT "ok   DiscoverPluginNames finds an installed .abas plugin, sorted"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL DiscoverPluginNames did not find the installed plugin"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    loadResult = LoadOnePlugin_TestOverride(pluginsDir, "gittools")
    IF loadResult.Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   a real plugin file loads successfully through the persistent plugin interpreter"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL gittools plugin failed to load: " + loadResult.Error
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    segmentOutput = Shell.PluginInvoke("prompt.segment", "git", {})
    IF segmentOutput == "git:main" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the plugin's registered prompt.segment callback is invoked correctly"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL prompt.segment invoke returned: [" + segmentOutput + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    commandOutput = Shell.PluginInvoke("command", "gitstatus", {"Arguments": []})
    IF commandOutput == "nothing to commit" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the plugin's registered command callback is invoked correctly"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL command invoke returned: [" + commandOutput + "]"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    themeValue = Shell.PluginCapabilityValue("theme", "neon")
    IF TYPEOF(themeValue) == "Object" THEN
        PRINT "ok   the plugin's registered theme capability is retrievable"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL theme capability was not retrievable"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    grouped = GroupCapabilitiesByPlugin(Shell.PluginCapabilities())
    gitToolsCaps = Object.Get(grouped, "gittools", [])
    groupedOk = LEN(gitToolsCaps) == 3
    IF groupedOk THEN groupedOk = Array.Contains(gitToolsCaps, "prompt.segment.git")
    IF groupedOk THEN groupedOk = Array.Contains(gitToolsCaps, "command.gitstatus")
    IF groupedOk THEN groupedOk = Array.Contains(gitToolsCaps, "theme.neon")
    IF groupedOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   GroupCapabilitiesByPlugin groups every capability under the registering plugin's name"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL capabilities were not grouped correctly under \"gittools\""
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ' AP-0052-016's own explicit "plugin failure" required test area: a deliberately broken plugin
    ' (a syntax error) must fail cleanly with a real error message, never crash the process, and
    ' never prevent a DIFFERENT, valid plugin from loading.
    brokenPlugin = "FUNCTION Broken(" + Chr(10) + "    this is not valid ArcoBASIC"
    ignored = File.WriteText(pluginsDir + "/broken.abas", brokenPlugin)
    brokenResult = LoadOnePlugin_TestOverride(pluginsDir, "broken")
    IF !brokenResult.Ok ANDALSO brokenResult.Error <> "" THEN
        PRINT "ok   a broken plugin fails to load with a real, non-empty error message"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a broken plugin did not report a clean failure"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    missingResult = LoadOnePlugin_TestOverride(pluginsDir, "not-a-real-plugin")
    IF !missingResult.Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   loading a not-installed plugin name fails cleanly instead of crashing"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL loading a missing plugin did not fail as expected"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    unknownOk = !Shell.PluginHasCapability("command", "not-a-real-command")
    IF unknownOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   an unregistered capability correctly reports as not present"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL an unregistered capability was incorrectly reported as present"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    ignored = Process.Execute("rm", ["-rf", testHome])
    ignored = Shell.PluginResetForTesting()
    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' Test-only variants of DiscoverPluginNames/LoadOnePlugin that take an explicit plugins directory
' rather than reading PluginsDir()/ProfileHome()/$ARCOSH_HOME -- keeps this self-test from ever
' touching the real user's own ~/.arcosh regardless of what ARCOSH_HOME happens to be set to when
' `--selftest-plugin` is run directly (unlike the profile/theme self-test, which can safely rely on
' ARCOSH_HOME since it never LOADS anything through the process-lifetime plugin interpreter that
' every OTHER selftest in this same process run would then also observe).
FUNCTION DiscoverPluginNames_TestOverride(pluginsDir AS STRING)
    names = []
    listed = Directory.List(pluginsDir)
    FOR entry IN listed.Entries
        IF String.EndsWith(entry, ".abas") THEN
            ignored = Array.Push(names, String.Slice(entry, 0, LEN(entry) - 5))
        END IF
    NEXT
    RETURN Array.Sort(names)
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION LoadOnePlugin_TestOverride(pluginsDir AS STRING, name AS STRING)
    path = pluginsDir + "/" + name + ".abas"
    IF !File.Exists(path) THEN
        RETURN {"Name": name, "Ok": FALSE, "Error": "plugin file not found: " + path}
    END IF
    fullSource = PluginPrelude() + "\n" + File.ReadText(path)
    result = Shell.LoadPlugin(name, fullSource)
    RETURN {"Name": name, "Ok": result.Ok, "Error": result.Error}
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Resident ArcoBASIC Program (RFC-0052 WP-006)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' RFC section 8 (Input Classification): a numbered program line is recognized BEFORE shell-command
' parsing ever runs -- classic BASIC line-number syntax isn't shell-word-splitting syntax (it has
' its own grammar), so this scans the raw trimmed input directly rather than reusing
' TokenizeCommand/ParseCommand for something they were never meant to parse.
FUNCTION ParseLeadingLineNumber(command AS STRING)
    i = 0
    length = LEN(command)
    digits = ""
    WHILE i < length
        ch = String.Slice(command, i, 1)
        IF !String.Contains("0123456789", ch) THEN EXIT WHILE
        digits = digits + ch
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    IF digits == "" THEN RETURN {"IsNumbered": FALSE, "Number": -1, "Rest": command}

    rest = String.Trim(String.Slice(command, i, length))
    RETURN {"IsNumbered": TRUE, "Number": NUMBER(digits), "Rest": rest}
END FUNCTION

' RFC section 8's "Input Classification" lists FOUR categories, not three: Shell command,
' ArcoBASIC immediate input, Numbered ArcoBASIC program line, Shell built-in/control command --
' AP-0052-009 explicitly requires proving "immediate ArcoBASIC input where supported" as part of
' WP-006, alongside the resident-program editor above. Classified on the ALREADY-parsed structure
' (never re-tokenizing) by shape, not by trying to compile the line and seeing if it happens to
' parse: a bare `ls` is syntactically a perfectly valid ArcoBASIC expression-statement (an
' identifier reference) that would only fail at RUNTIME ("undefined variable: ls") -- parsing it
' successfully and only discovering the mismatch after already committing to the ArcoBASIC path
' would make ordinary commands like bare `ls` throw a confusing ArcoBASIC error instead of running
' the real program every real shell user expects. A leading ArcoBASIC statement keyword (`PRINT`,
' `IF`, ...) or a plain/compound assignment shape (`x = ...`, `x += ...`) can never legitimately be
' the start of a real Linux command line, so those are the only two shapes trusted here.
FUNCTION IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(parsed AS OBJECT)
    IF parsed.Executable == "" THEN RETURN FALSE
    leadingKeywords = ["PRINT", "LET", "IF", "SELECT", "WHILE", "DO", "FOR", "FUNCTION", "RETURN",
        "TRY", "THROW", "GOTO", "STOP", "CLASS", "INTERFACE", "FLAGS"]
    IF Array.Contains(leadingKeywords, Upper(parsed.Executable)) THEN RETURN TRUE
    IF LEN(parsed.Arguments) > 0 THEN
        assignmentOperators = ["=", "+=", "-=", "*=", "/="]
        IF Array.Contains(assignmentOperators, parsed.Arguments[0]) THEN RETURN TRUE
    END IF
    RETURN FALSE
END FUNCTION

' Runs ONE line of immediate ArcoBASIC input for real (Runtime.EvalImmediate, arco_runtime --
' variables persist across separate calls, unlike Runtime.RunString's fresh-Runtime-per-call
' resident-program RUN just below). Takes the RAW trimmed command text, never anything rebuilt
' from `parsed`'s own tokens -- TokenizeCommand strips quotes the way ordinary shell word-splitting
' does (correct for a real command's arguments), which would silently turn `PRINT "hello"` into
' `PRINT hello` (a bare, undefined-variable identifier, not the string literal it has to stay) if
' this reconstructed the source from parsed.Executable/Arguments instead of using the original text
' directly.
FUNCTION RunImmediateInput(command AS STRING)
    result = Runtime.EvalImmediate(command)
    IF !result.Ok THEN ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: " + result.Error)
END FUNCTION

' RFC section 9's own rules: new numbered line -> insert; existing numbered line -> replace; bare
' line number (empty `text`) -> delete; lines remain numerically ordered. A reusable abstraction
' (this function plus the plain {Number, Text} array it operates on) rather than the old, retired
' arcosh.cpp's inline, non-reusable REPL-local version -- see RFC section 9's own note on this and
' the WP-000 audit finding it confirmed.
FUNCTION SetProgramLine(program AS ARRAY, lineNumber AS NUMBER, text AS STRING)
    existingIndex = -1
    insertAt = LEN(program)
    i = 0
    WHILE i < LEN(program)
        existing = program[i]
        IF existing.Number == lineNumber THEN
            existingIndex = i
            insertAt = i
            EXIT WHILE
        END IF
        IF existing.Number > lineNumber THEN
            insertAt = i
            EXIT WHILE
        END IF
        i = i + 1
    WEND

    IF existingIndex >= 0 THEN ignored = Array.RemoveAt(program, existingIndex)

    IF text <> "" THEN
        ignored = Array.Insert(program, insertAt, {"Number": lineNumber, "Text": text})
    END IF
END FUNCTION

' Structural regression test for RFC-0052 AP-0052-009's own required scenario (section 9's exact
' example): insert, replace, delete-by-bare-number, and out-of-order insertion staying numerically
' ordered -- plus one real RUN through Runtime.RunString, proving the resident program actually
' executes (GOTO included), not just that the line buffer itself is maintained correctly.
FUNCTION RunResidentProgramSelfTest()
    failures = 0
    program = []

    line1 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("10 PRINT \"ONE\"")
    SetProgramLine(program, line1.Number, line1.Rest)
    line2 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("20 PRINT \"TWO\"")
    SetProgramLine(program, line2.Number, line2.Rest)

    IF LEN(program) == 2 THEN
        PRINT "ok   initial insert count == 2"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL initial insert count == " + STRING(LEN(program)) + " (expected 2)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    line3 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("20 PRINT \"THREE\"")
    SetProgramLine(program, line3.Number, line3.Rest)

    IF LEN(program) == 2 THEN
        PRINT "ok   replace keeps count == 2"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL replace count == " + STRING(LEN(program)) + " (expected 2)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF program[1].Text == "PRINT \"THREE\"" THEN
        PRINT "ok   line 20 replaced with THREE"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL line 20 text == " + program[1].Text + " (expected PRINT \"THREE\")"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    lineDel = ParseLeadingLineNumber("20")
    SetProgramLine(program, lineDel.Number, lineDel.Rest)

    IF LEN(program) == 1 THEN
        PRINT "ok   delete-by-bare-number leaves count == 1"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL delete count == " + STRING(LEN(program)) + " (expected 1)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    IF program[0].Number == 10 THEN
        PRINT "ok   remaining line is 10"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL remaining line number == " + STRING(program[0].Number) + " (expected 10)"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    program2 = []
    a = ParseLeadingLineNumber("30 PRINT \"C\"")
    SetProgramLine(program2, a.Number, a.Rest)
    b = ParseLeadingLineNumber("10 PRINT \"A\"")
    SetProgramLine(program2, b.Number, b.Rest)
    c = ParseLeadingLineNumber("20 PRINT \"B\"")
    SetProgramLine(program2, c.Number, c.Rest)

    orderedOk = TRUE
    IF program2[0].Number <> 10 THEN orderedOk = FALSE
    IF program2[1].Number <> 20 THEN orderedOk = FALSE
    IF program2[2].Number <> 30 THEN orderedOk = FALSE
    IF orderedOk THEN
        PRINT "ok   numerical ordering after out-of-order insert"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL numerical ordering: " + STRING(program2[0].Number) + "," + STRING(program2[1].Number) + "," + STRING(program2[2].Number)
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    runProgram = []
    r1 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("10 x = 1")
    SetProgramLine(runProgram, r1.Number, r1.Rest)
    r2 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("20 x = x + 1")
    SetProgramLine(runProgram, r2.Number, r2.Rest)
    r3 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("30 IF x < 3 THEN GOTO 20")
    SetProgramLine(runProgram, r3.Number, r3.Rest)
    r4 = ParseLeadingLineNumber("40 PRINT \"resident-run-ok:\" + STRING(x)")
    SetProgramLine(runProgram, r4.Number, r4.Rest)

    lines = []
    FOR entry IN runProgram
        ignored = Array.Push(lines, STRING(entry.Number) + " " + entry.Text)
    NEXT
    runResult = Runtime.RunString(String.Join(lines, "\n"))
    IF runResult.Ok THEN
        PRINT "ok   RUN executed the resident program (GOTO loop included) without error"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL RUN error: " + runResult.Error
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Script Execution (RFC-0052 WP-007)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

' `RUN <file>` (a real path, host or Arcology-colon-style -- Path.ToHost handles either, see the
' Path Layer's own header comment). Reads the raw file text and hands it directly to
' Runtime.RunString -- the SAME canonical compiler/parser path every other ArcoBASIC program goes
' through, never a hand-rolled preprocessor for numbered source (AP-0052-010's own explicit
' requirement). Numbered lines, unnumbered lines, and a mix of both in one file all just work
' because Parser::statement() (src/frontend/parser.cpp) already accepts an optional leading
' line-number label on ANY statement, not only inside some special "classic mode" -- confirmed
' directly against three fixture files (arcosh/tests/fixtures/wp007_*.abas, exercised by
' RunScriptExecutionSelfTest below): pure unnumbered structured code, a classic numbered GOTO
' loop, and a file mixing an unnumbered FUNCTION declaration with numbered top-level statements
' that call it, all producing correct results with zero special-casing here.
FUNCTION RunScriptFile(path AS STRING)
    hostPath = Path.ToHost(path)
    IF !File.Exists(hostPath) THEN
        RETURN {"Ok": FALSE, "Error": "no such file: " + path, "Exited": FALSE, "ExitCode": 0}
    END IF
    source = File.ReadText(hostPath)
    RETURN Runtime.RunString(source)
END FUNCTION

' Each fixture calls ExitTheProgram with its own distinctive code so this can verify the RIGHT
' program actually ran (Ok alone only proves SOME valid program ran without erroring) without
' needing to capture PRINT output or touch the filesystem from inside the nested Runtime. Nested
' IF rather than ANDALSO throughout -- Ok/Exited are Boxed booleans (object fields off
' Runtime.RunString's returned OBJECT), and this file avoids combining a Boxed bool with
' ANDALSO/ORELSE everywhere else too (see the is_tty comment above for the disclosed reason).
FUNCTION AssertScriptRanOk(label AS STRING, outcome AS OBJECT, expectedExitCode)
    IF !outcome.Ok THEN
        PRINT "FAIL " + label + ": " + outcome.Error
        RETURN FALSE
    END IF
    IF !outcome.Exited THEN
        PRINT "FAIL " + label + ": script finished without calling ExitTheProgram"
        RETURN FALSE
    END IF
    IF outcome.ExitCode <> expectedExitCode THEN
        PRINT "FAIL " + label + ": exit code " + STRING(outcome.ExitCode) + " (expected " + STRING(expectedExitCode) + ")"
        RETURN FALSE
    END IF
    PRINT "ok   " + label
    RETURN TRUE
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RunScriptExecutionSelfTest(fixturesDir AS STRING)
    failures = 0

    missing = RunScriptFile(fixturesDir + "/does-not-exist.abas")
    IF missing.Ok THEN
        PRINT "FAIL RunScriptFile reported Ok for a nonexistent file"
        failures = failures + 1
    ELSE
        PRINT "ok   RunScriptFile reports a clean error for a nonexistent file"
    END IF

    IF !AssertScriptRanOk("unnumbered .abas source ran through the canonical compiler path", RunScriptFile(fixturesDir + "/wp007_unnumbered.abas"), 41) THEN
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !AssertScriptRanOk("classic numbered GOTO source ran through the canonical compiler path", RunScriptFile(fixturesDir + "/wp007_numbered.abas"), 42) THEN
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF
    IF !AssertScriptRanOk("mixed numbered/unnumbered source ran through the canonical compiler path", RunScriptFile(fixturesDir + "/wp007_mixed.abas"), 43) THEN
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

' AP-0052-009's own "immediate ArcoBASIC input where supported" requirement (RFC section 8).
FUNCTION RunImmediateInputSelfTest()
    failures = 0

    printParsed = ParseCommand("PRINT \"x\"")
    IF IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(printParsed) THEN
        PRINT "ok   a PRINT statement classifies as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a PRINT statement did not classify as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    assignParsed = ParseCommand("total = 5")
    IF IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(assignParsed) THEN
        PRINT "ok   a plain assignment classifies as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a plain assignment did not classify as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    compoundParsed = ParseCommand("total += 5")
    IF IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(compoundParsed) THEN
        PRINT "ok   a compound assignment classifies as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
    ELSE
        PRINT "FAIL a compound assignment did not classify as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    lsParsed = ParseCommand("ls -la /tmp")
    IF IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(lsParsed) THEN
        PRINT "FAIL a bare external command misclassified as ArcoBASIC immediate input"
        failures = failures + 1
    ELSE
        PRINT "ok   a bare external command classifies as a shell command, not ArcoBASIC"
    END IF

    ' Real execution, not just classification -- and persistence across two SEPARATE calls, the
    ' actual point of Runtime.EvalImmediate over Runtime.RunString: a real REPL-immediate-mode
    ' variable set on one line must still be visible on the next.
    setResult = Runtime.EvalImmediate("__wp006_immediate_selftest_var = 41 + 1")
    IF !setResult.Ok THEN
        PRINT "FAIL Runtime.EvalImmediate assignment failed: " + setResult.Error
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    checkResult = Runtime.EvalImmediate(
        "IF __wp006_immediate_selftest_var == 42 THEN ExitTheProgram(77) ELSE ExitTheProgram(78)")
    persisted = FALSE
    IF checkResult.Exited THEN
        IF checkResult.ExitCode == 77 THEN persisted = TRUE
    END IF
    IF persisted THEN
        PRINT "ok   a variable set by one immediate-input line persisted to the next"
    ELSE
        failMessage = "FAIL variable did not persist across separate Runtime.EvalImmediate calls ("
        failMessage = failMessage + "Ok=" + STRING(checkResult.Ok) + " Exited=" + STRING(checkResult.Exited)
        failMessage = failMessage + " ExitCode=" + STRING(checkResult.ExitCode) + " Error=" + checkResult.Error + ")"
        PRINT failMessage
        failures = failures + 1
    END IF

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

FUNCTION RunPathLayerSelfTest()
    cases = [
        [":", "/"],
        [":home:", "/home/"],
        [":home:user:", "/home/user/"],
        [":home:user:Desktop:", "/home/user/Desktop/"],
        [":home:user:Desktop:file.txt", "/home/user/Desktop/file.txt"],
        ["  :home:user:  ", "/home/user/"],
        ["\":home:user:\"", "/home/user/"],
        [":home:user:My Documents:", "/home/user/My Documents/"],
        [":home:user:..:other:", "/home/other/"],
        [":home:.:user:", "/home/user/"],
        ["::", "/"],
        ["/usr/bin", "/usr/bin"],
        ["./relative", "./relative"]
    ]

    failures = 0
    FOR testCase IN cases
        input = testCase[0]
        expected = testCase[1]
        actual = Path.ToHost(input)
        IF actual == expected THEN
            PRINT "ok   Path.ToHost(" + input + ") == " + expected
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL Path.ToHost(" + input + ") == " + actual + " (expected " + expected + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    NEXT

    roundtrips = [
        ["/home/user/", ":home:user:"],
        ["/", ":"],
        ["relative/path", "relative/path"]
    ]
    FOR testCase IN roundtrips
        input = testCase[0]
        expected = testCase[1]
        actual = Path.ToArcology(input)
        IF actual == expected THEN
            PRINT "ok   Path.ToArcology(" + input + ") == " + expected
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL Path.ToArcology(" + input + ") == " + actual + " (expected " + expected + ")"
            failures = failures + 1
        END IF
    NEXT

    RETURN failures
END FUNCTION

version = "0.15.0-plugins"
no_color_flag = HasFlag(Args, "--no-color")
no_color_env = Process.Env("NO_COLOR") <> ""
term = Process.Env("TERM")
color_term = Process.Env("COLORTERM")
is_tty = Console.IsTTY()

' NOTE: this backend's generic host-function bridge (src/native/host_bridge.cpp) has a real,
' disclosed bug where a bool RETURNED DIRECTLY FROM A HOST FUNCTION crashes if fed straight into
' ANDALSO/ORELSE (confirmed with plain File.Exists() too, not specific to Console.IsTTY -- see
' arcology-os/rfcs/RFC-0053... progress notes). A bare `IF is_tty THEN` / `IF !is_tty THEN` is
' fine; only the short-circuit combination is affected. Worked around here with nested IF instead
' of ANDALSO wherever a host-returned bool (is_tty) is involved; comparison-expression results
' (term <> "dumb", String.Slice(...) == ":", etc.) combine via ANDALSO/ORELSE without issue
' throughout the rest of this file.
' RFC-0052 section 22 (Configuration): ~/.arcosh/config's "NoColor" setting is a persisted
' fallback, checked here so it can WIDEN the no-color decision (a saved preference turning color
' off) but never NARROW an explicit --no-color/NO_COLOR back on -- the ordinary Unix convention of
' an explicit flag/environment override beating a saved one. ApplyStartupConfig's own return value
' is a Boxed object-field read (config.NoColor -- Kind::Index always classifies Boxed, regardless
' of the underlying value), so it's combined with nested IF here, never ANDALSO/ORELSE directly,
' for the same disclosed reason is_tty's own detection above does the same.
config_no_color = ApplyStartupConfig()
effective_no_color_flag = no_color_flag
IF config_no_color THEN effective_no_color_flag = TRUE

color_tier = DetectColorTier(is_tty, effective_no_color_flag, no_color_env, term, color_term)
ignored = Runtime.SetGlobal("arcosh_color_tier", color_tier)

IF HasFlag(Args, "--version") THEN
    PRINT "arcosh " + version + " (RFC-0052 WP-001..WP-012, WP-008, WP-009, HELP, Profiles, Editors, Prompt Color Tags, Date/Time Fields, User Segment)"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--diagnostic") THEN
    PRINT "ArcoSH diagnostic"
    PRINT "  version:        " + version
    PRINT "  cwd (host):     " + Path.Cwd()
    PRINT "  cwd (arcology): " + Path.ToArcology(Path.Cwd())
    PRINT "  stdout is tty:  " + STRING(is_tty)
    PRINT "  TERM:           " + term
    PRINT "  COLORTERM:      " + color_term
    PRINT "  NO_COLOR set:   " + STRING(no_color_env)
    PRINT "  --no-color:     " + STRING(no_color_flag)
    PRINT "  color tier:     " + color_tier
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-paths") THEN
    failures = RunPathLayerSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " path layer test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all path layer tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-oops") THEN
    failures = RunOopsSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " oops test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all oops tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-resident") THEN
    failures = RunResidentProgramSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " resident program test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all resident program tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-script-execution") THEN
    fixturesDir = ArgumentAfterFlag(Args, "--selftest-script-execution")
    failures = RunScriptExecutionSelfTest(fixturesDir)
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " script execution test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all script execution tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-pipeline") THEN
    failures = RunPipelineSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " pipeline/redirection test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all pipeline/redirection tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-immediate") THEN
    failures = RunImmediateInputSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " immediate-input test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all immediate-input tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-jobcontrol") THEN
    failures = RunJobControlSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " job control test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all job control tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-display") THEN
    failures = RunDisplaySelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " display/theme test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all display/theme tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-help") THEN
    failures = RunHelpSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " help test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all help tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-profile") THEN
    failures = RunProfileSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " profile/theme/prompt test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all profile/theme/prompt tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-editor") THEN
    failures = RunEditorSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " theme/prompt editor test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all theme/prompt editor tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

IF HasFlag(Args, "--selftest-plugin") THEN
    failures = RunPluginSelfTest()
    IF failures > 0 THEN
        PRINT STRING(failures) + " plugin test(s) failed"
        ExitTheProgram(1)
    END IF
    PRINT "all plugin tests passed"
    ExitTheProgram(0)
END IF

' Reduced terminal capability (piped stdin, dumb terminal, no color) must degrade gracefully
' rather than fail -- RFC-0052 section 17/18 (Display and Themes, WP-008): the banner uses the
' real `prompt` role through Display.Style/Reset like everything else in this file now does,
' which itself already degrades to plain text at tier None, so the explicit "[no-color]" suffix
' below is purely an extra, redundant confirmation for the human reading it, not something the
' styling itself depends on.
ignored = Display.Style("prompt")
ignored = Display.Text("Arcology Shell (arcosh) " + version)
ignored = Display.Reset()
IF color_tier == "None" THEN
    PRINT " [no-color]"
ELSE
    PRINT ""
END IF

IF !is_tty THEN
    PRINT "(non-interactive input; reading commands from stdin)"
END IF

' RFC-0052 WP-012: claims the controlling terminal for this process group and leaves
' SIGINT/SIGQUIT/SIGTSTP/SIGTTIN/SIGTTOU ignored for the shell's own entire interactive lifetime
' (never saved-and-restored per command) -- see Process.SetupShellJobControl's own much larger
' comment in runtime.cpp. Safe to call even non-interactively (piped stdin, ctest): it detects that
' itself and is a no-op.
ignored = Process.SetupShellJobControl()

' RFC-0052 section 19.3's own required "recovery launch with optional plugins disabled" -- a
' broken plugin found no other way is never a reason arcosh itself becomes unreachable. Plugins
' are loaded AFTER color/theme startup config is already applied (not before): a persisted config
' naming a plugin-contributed theme as the default therefore falls back to DefaultTheme() for THIS
' one startup rather than resolving correctly -- a real, narrow, disclosed limitation (see "help
' plugins") rather than reordering startup and risking the color/diagnostic/--selftest-* fast
' exits above, none of which should ever depend on plugin loading having already happened.
IF !HasFlag(Args, "--no-plugins") THEN
    ignored = LoadEnabledPlugins()
ELSE
    ignored = DisplayMuted("arcosh: plugins disabled for this session (--no-plugins)")
END IF

running = TRUE
history = []
resident_program = []
jobs_table = []
last_exit_code = 0
WHILE running
    ' A background job finishing or stopping on its own schedule is reported here, before the
    ' next prompt is drawn -- never mid-command, and never by blocking the prompt to find out.
    ignored = PollBackgroundJobs(jobs_table)

    cwd = Path.Cwd()
    IF is_tty THEN Console.Write(Prompt(cwd, last_exit_code, jobs_table))

    line = Console.ReadLine()
    IF !line.Ok THEN
        IF is_tty THEN PRINT ""
        running = FALSE
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    command = String.Trim(line.Text)
    IF command == "" THEN CONTINUE WHILE

    ' RFC section 8 (Input Classification): a numbered program line is recognized before shell-
    ' command parsing ever runs -- it isn't shell syntax, so it never reaches ParseCommand at all.
    lineNumberInfo = ParseLeadingLineNumber(command)
    IF lineNumberInfo.IsNumbered THEN
        SetProgramLine(resident_program, lineNumberInfo.Number, lineNumberInfo.Rest)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    parsed = ParseCommand(command)

    ' RFC section 12's resolution order starts with "protected ArcoSH built-ins" -- checked first,
    ' against the PARSED executable name (not the raw string), so quoting/extra-whitespace behave
    ' the same way for a builtin as for any real command. Every branch records exactly one
    ' history entry (RFC-0052 WP-005) -- built-ins included, for a consistent, inspectable record.
    IF parsed.Executable == "exit" ORELSE parsed.Executable == "EXIT" ORELSE parsed.Executable == "quit" THEN
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 0))
        running = FALSE
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "pwd" THEN
        PRINT Path.Cwd()
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 0))
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "history" THEN
        index = 0
        FOR entry IN history
            PRINT STRING(index) + ": " + entry.SourceText
            index = index + 1
        NEXT
        ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 0))
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "cd" THEN
        target = Path.Home()
        IF LEN(parsed.Arguments) > 0 THEN target = parsed.Arguments[0]
        hostTarget = Path.ToHost(target)
        changeResult = Directory.Change(hostTarget)
        IF changeResult.Ok THEN
            ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "SUCCESS", 0))
        ELSE
            ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: cd: " + target + ": " + changeResult.Error)
            ignored = Array.Push(history, MakeHistoryEntry(parsed, "PATH_NOT_FOUND", -1))
        END IF
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "oops" THEN
        last_exit_code = RunOops(history, jobs_table, parsed)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    ' RFC-0052 section 23 (Job Control). `jobs`/`fg`/`bg` are lowercase like every other UNIX-style
    ' shell built-in above (cd/pwd/exit/history/oops), not uppercase like the classic-BASIC
    ' LIST/RUN/NEW keywords below -- they're shell vocabulary, not ArcoBASIC language keywords.
    IF parsed.Executable == "jobs" THEN
        ignored = RunJobsCommand(jobs_table)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "fg" THEN
        last_exit_code = RunForegroundResume(history, jobs_table, parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "bg" THEN
        ignored = RunBackgroundResume(jobs_table, parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "help" ORELSE parsed.Executable == "?" THEN
        ignored = RunHelpCommand(parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    ' RFC-0052 sections 20/22 (Prompt Architecture / Configuration).
    IF parsed.Executable == "theme" THEN
        ignored = RunThemeCommand(parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "prompt" THEN
        ignored = RunPromptCommand(cwd, parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "profile" THEN
        ignored = RunProfileCommand(parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "plugins" THEN
        ignored = RunPluginsCommand(parsed.Arguments)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    ' RFC-0052 WP-009: a registered "command" plugin capability is checked as its own shell
    ' built-in, BEFORE falling through to real Linux process execution below -- so a plugin command
    ' takes precedence over a same-named real program on PATH, a disclosed, deliberate tradeoff
    ' (see "help plugins") in exchange for never touching the pipeline/redirection/background
    ' machinery at all for a plugin command (a real, disclosed scope limit, not an oversight).
    IF Shell.PluginHasCapability("command", parsed.Executable) THEN
        ignored = RunPluginCommand(parsed)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    ' RFC-0052 WP-006: classic BASIC keywords, checked case-insensitively (LIST/RUN/NEW are always
    ' uppercase by RFC section 9's own examples, unlike the lowercase UNIX-style shell built-ins
    ' above) -- not recorded into `history`, which RFC section 14 scopes to shell commands, not
    ' resident-program editing.
    IF Upper(parsed.Executable) == "LIST" THEN
        FOR entry IN resident_program
            PRINT STRING(entry.Number) + " " + entry.Text
        NEXT
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF Upper(parsed.Executable) == "RUN" THEN
        IF LEN(parsed.Arguments) > 0 THEN
            scriptResult = RunScriptFile(parsed.Arguments[0])
            IF !scriptResult.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: run: " + scriptResult.Error)
            END IF
            CONTINUE WHILE
        END IF
        IF LEN(resident_program) > 0 THEN
            lines = []
            FOR entry IN resident_program
                ignored = Array.Push(lines, STRING(entry.Number) + " " + entry.Text)
            NEXT
            runResult = Runtime.RunString(String.Join(lines, "\n"))
            IF !runResult.Ok THEN
                ignored = DisplayError("arcosh: run: " + runResult.Error)
            END IF
        END IF
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF Upper(parsed.Executable) == "NEW" THEN
        resident_program = []
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    ' RFC-0052 section 8 (Input Classification) / AP-0052-009: unnumbered ArcoBASIC input typed
    ' directly at the prompt runs immediately, its own category distinct from a shell command --
    ' checked after every classic-BASIC/shell built-in above (so e.g. bare `RUN` still means "run
    ' the resident program", not ArcoBASIC's own RUN statement) and before background/pipeline
    ' handling below, which has no meaning for an ArcoBASIC statement.
    IF IsArcoBasicImmediateInput(parsed) THEN
        ignored = RunImmediateInput(command)
        CONTINUE WHILE
    END IF

    IF parsed.Executable == "" THEN CONTINUE WHILE

    ' RFC-0052 WP-012: `command &` starts a real, job-table-tracked background job
    ' (RunBackgroundJob) rather than being refused; everything else -- a single command or a real
    ' pipeline, with or without redirection -- goes through RunForegroundJob, which now handles
    ' suspend/resume (Ctrl-Z) for ANY foreground command, not just something explicitly
    ' backgrounded. See RunForegroundJob's own comment for why there is only one code path left
    ' here instead of separate plain-command/pipeline branches.
    IF parsed.Background THEN
        last_exit_code = RunBackgroundJob(history, jobs_table, parsed)
    ELSE
        last_exit_code = RunForegroundJob(history, jobs_table, parsed)
    END IF
WEND

ExitTheProgram(0)
